1
00:00:05,005 --> 00:00:08,241
♪ In the not too distant future

2
00:00:08,274 --> 00:00:10,577
♪ Next Sunday A.D.

3
00:00:10,610 --> 00:00:14,481
♪ There was a guy named Joel

4
00:00:14,514 --> 00:00:17,584
♪ Not too different
from you or me

5
00:00:17,617 --> 00:00:20,487
♪ He worked at
Gizmonic Institute

6
00:00:20,520 --> 00:00:23,490
♪ Just another face
in a red jumpsuit

7
00:00:23,523 --> 00:00:26,593
♪ He did a good job
cleaning up the place

8
00:00:26,626 --> 00:00:28,428
♪ But his bosses didn't like him

9
00:00:28,461 --> 00:00:30,797
♪ So they shot him into space

10
00:00:32,966 --> 00:00:36,269
♪ We'll send him cheesy movies

11
00:00:36,302 --> 00:00:38,438
♪ The worst we can find

12
00:00:38,471 --> 00:00:39,606
♪ La la la

13
00:00:39,639 --> 00:00:42,275
♪ He'll have to sit
and watch them all

14
00:00:42,308 --> 00:00:44,944
♪ And we'll monitor his mind

15
00:00:44,978 --> 00:00:45,979
♪ La la la

16
00:00:46,012 --> 00:00:48,481
♪ Now keep in mind
Joel can't control

17
00:00:48,515 --> 00:00:50,950
♪ Where the movies begin or end

18
00:00:50,984 --> 00:00:51,951
♪ La la la

19
00:00:51,985 --> 00:00:54,788
♪ Because he used
those special parts

20
00:00:54,821 --> 00:00:58,324
♪ To make his robot friends

21
00:00:59,559 --> 00:01:01,494
♪ Robot roll call

22
00:01:01,528 --> 00:01:02,629
♪ Cambot, pan left

23
00:01:02,662 --> 00:01:04,297
♪ Gypsy, hi girl

24
00:01:04,330 --> 00:01:05,632
♪ Tom Servo, what a cool guy

25
00:01:05,665 --> 00:01:07,300
♪ Croooow, he's a wisecracker

26
00:01:07,333 --> 00:01:10,503
♪ If you're wondering
how he eats and breathes

27
00:01:10,537 --> 00:01:12,639
♪ And other science facts

28
00:01:12,672 --> 00:01:13,973
♪ La la la

29
00:01:14,007 --> 00:01:16,476
♪ Then repeat to
yourself, it's just a show

30
00:01:16,509 --> 00:01:19,512
♪ I should really just relax

31
00:01:19,546 --> 00:01:25,018
♪ For Mystery Science
Theater 3000

32
00:01:25,051 --> 00:01:30,023
(doors slamming)

33
00:01:32,859 --> 00:01:35,028
Hey, hi, welcome to
the Satellite of Love.

34
00:01:35,061 --> 00:01:37,063
I'm Joel Robinson.
These are my robots.

35
00:01:37,097 --> 00:01:40,333
I want you to consider
the lowly waffle.

36
00:01:40,366 --> 00:01:41,701
Joel, it's lunch time, buddy.

37
00:01:41,734 --> 00:01:44,204
I'd rather have a Manwich
or a baloney sandwich.

38
00:01:44,237 --> 00:01:45,505
Oh, nonsense, Tom.

39
00:01:45,538 --> 00:01:47,407
Anytime is the right
time for waffles.

40
00:01:47,440 --> 00:01:49,008
Joel, I think
the butter slipped

41
00:01:49,042 --> 00:01:50,677
off your stack of
waffles, buddy.

42
00:01:50,710 --> 00:01:52,178
- Are you feeling okay?
- Sure.

43
00:01:52,212 --> 00:01:53,880
Commercial sign
in 15 seconds.

44
00:01:53,913 --> 00:01:55,181
What are you making
there, partner?

45
00:01:55,215 --> 00:01:57,550
I'm making waffles and
save room, magic voice.

46
00:01:57,584 --> 00:01:59,185
- I made plenty.
- I'll say.

47
00:01:59,219 --> 00:02:01,187
Consider the waffle
as a fine and suitable

48
00:02:01,221 --> 00:02:03,890
alternative to
stuffing or potatoes.

49
00:02:03,923 --> 00:02:06,593
Because after all, what
are waffles but batter?

50
00:02:06,626 --> 00:02:08,528
And what's batter
but chopped up grain.

51
00:02:08,561 --> 00:02:11,064
You know there's tons of
stuff you can do with waffles.

52
00:02:11,097 --> 00:02:13,032
You can take some
peanut butter and syrup,

53
00:02:13,066 --> 00:02:14,868
and make a waffle sandwich.

54
00:02:14,901 --> 00:02:17,570
Or wrap the hot dog in a waffle

55
00:02:17,604 --> 00:02:18,938
- and garnish with ketchup.
- Ugh!

56
00:02:18,972 --> 00:02:20,039
There's tons you can do.
(groaning)

57
00:02:20,073 --> 00:02:21,374
Or maybe some Swiss cheese...

58
00:02:21,407 --> 00:02:24,410
Five, four, three,
two, commercial sign out.

59
00:02:24,444 --> 00:02:25,545
Beans are always good...

60
00:02:25,578 --> 00:02:30,416
(Mystery Science Theater
3000 theme music)

61
00:02:31,584 --> 00:02:33,453
Well, I like to
put chili on mine,

62
00:02:33,486 --> 00:02:35,121
for a real south of
the border treat.

63
00:02:35,155 --> 00:02:36,723
- And if you're really into it,
- No...

64
00:02:36,756 --> 00:02:39,392
Take some waffle batter
and mix it with milk,

65
00:02:39,425 --> 00:02:41,227
and you've got a waffle shake.

66
00:02:41,261 --> 00:02:43,396
But don't tell the kids
it's good for them.

67
00:02:43,429 --> 00:02:45,565
Or a delicious spinach waffle

68
00:02:45,598 --> 00:02:47,100
with tangy ricotta cheese.

69
00:02:47,133 --> 00:02:48,902
Joel, the mad
scientists are calling.

70
00:02:48,935 --> 00:02:50,570
I've got plenty for them too.

71
00:02:50,603 --> 00:02:51,337
I'll get it.

72
00:02:52,272 --> 00:02:53,306
Well, Aunt Jemima,

73
00:02:53,339 --> 00:02:55,575
this time you've really
stepped off the deep end.

74
00:02:55,608 --> 00:02:56,776
Frank?

75
00:02:56,809 --> 00:02:57,911
Do you shred
the Swiss cheese,

76
00:02:57,944 --> 00:02:59,412
or just slice it really thin?

77
00:02:59,445 --> 00:03:03,283
Frank, this time I'm
really going to hurt you.

78
00:03:03,316 --> 00:03:04,751
I understand.

79
00:03:04,784 --> 00:03:05,919
Why don't you go and get

80
00:03:05,952 --> 00:03:07,587
this weeks invention
exchange, alright?

81
00:03:07,620 --> 00:03:10,123
Yes, sir. Thank you, sir.

82
00:03:10,790 --> 00:03:12,759
You've been very kind to me.

83
00:03:12,792 --> 00:03:15,428
Frank, you have no clue.

84
00:03:15,461 --> 00:03:16,829
Well, Aunt Jemima,

85
00:03:16,863 --> 00:03:18,765
our invention
exchange this week,

86
00:03:18,798 --> 00:03:21,434
has to do with meat.
Frank?

87
00:03:21,467 --> 00:03:24,003
Well, you know recently,
I've become a vegetarian.

88
00:03:24,037 --> 00:03:26,606
And it's worked
out great, really.

89
00:03:26,639 --> 00:03:27,840
You know, the other day my colon

90
00:03:27,874 --> 00:03:30,476
looked up at me and
said, Frank, thank you.

91
00:03:30,510 --> 00:03:32,946
I said, no thank you.

92
00:03:32,979 --> 00:03:34,147
But now what am I gonna do with

93
00:03:34,180 --> 00:03:36,316
all the meat I have
stored in freezers?

94
00:03:36,349 --> 00:03:38,785
I figured, hey, why not
bring the meat back to life?

95
00:03:38,818 --> 00:03:40,119
That's right,
that's why we've

96
00:03:40,153 --> 00:03:42,155
invented the meat re-animator.

97
00:03:42,188 --> 00:03:43,156
Hook it up Frank.

98
00:03:43,189 --> 00:03:44,324
Clear!

99
00:03:44,357 --> 00:03:45,625
(zapping)

100
00:03:45,658 --> 00:03:47,794
It's alive! Alive!

101
00:03:47,827 --> 00:03:50,363
My corn-fed, Minnesota
chicken is alive!

102
00:03:50,396 --> 00:03:52,832
You know, I thought
this was a good idea.

103
00:03:52,865 --> 00:03:55,802
But this is one weird mama-jama.

104
00:03:55,835 --> 00:03:57,804
What do you
think, Garrick Utley?

105
00:03:57,837 --> 00:03:58,871
Chicken waffles?

106
00:03:58,905 --> 00:03:59,806
Oh, you're weird.

107
00:03:59,839 --> 00:04:01,307
Which results in creativity.

108
00:04:01,341 --> 00:04:03,376
Which results in my
latest invention exchange.

109
00:04:03,409 --> 00:04:07,013
It's a very literal
interpretation of
the old waffle iron.

110
00:04:07,046 --> 00:04:09,482
Let's say you love
traditional waffles,

111
00:04:09,515 --> 00:04:11,551
but you like the
classic simplistic

112
00:04:11,584 --> 00:04:13,486
styling of the old pancake.

113
00:04:13,519 --> 00:04:14,854
No problem.

114
00:04:14,887 --> 00:04:18,157
Just douse it with a little
Mrs. Butterworth spray starch.

115
00:04:18,191 --> 00:04:19,325
- (coughing)
- Like that.

116
00:04:19,359 --> 00:04:22,662
Okay, cover with a
cloth to avoid burns.

117
00:04:22,695 --> 00:04:24,497
- Mm-hmm.
- And iron.

118
00:04:24,530 --> 00:04:25,531
- Say...
- Hey...

119
00:04:25,565 --> 00:04:26,933
And?

120
00:04:28,067 --> 00:04:31,571
Gee, it turns an ordinary
waffle into a flat waffle.

121
00:04:31,604 --> 00:04:32,739
And clean-up's a breeze.

122
00:04:32,772 --> 00:04:33,840
What do you think, sirs?

123
00:04:33,873 --> 00:04:35,708
Ancient Chinese secret, huh?

124
00:04:35,742 --> 00:04:37,677
Dear god, what have I done?

125
00:04:37,710 --> 00:04:39,746
What the heck is
going on up there?

126
00:04:39,779 --> 00:04:42,849
Hey, Frank, will
these work on waffles?

127
00:04:42,882 --> 00:04:44,083
Yeah, I think so.

128
00:04:44,117 --> 00:04:47,053
Well, Joel,
today's experiment

129
00:04:47,086 --> 00:04:49,689
is a little piece of
slime from Roger Corman,

130
00:04:49,722 --> 00:04:51,391
called Viking Women
and the Sea Serpent.

131
00:04:51,424 --> 00:04:55,595
But first, a little
lesson in home economics.

132
00:04:55,628 --> 00:04:57,363
Here's a waffle
for you, scarecrow.

133
00:04:57,397 --> 00:05:02,568
(alarm buzzing)
(everyone panicking)

134
00:05:04,570 --> 00:05:09,575
(doors grinding and slamming)

135
00:05:11,077 --> 00:05:12,945
I just like
waffles, that's all.

136
00:05:12,979 --> 00:05:14,781
Carried away, I'd say.

137
00:05:14,814 --> 00:05:17,383
(whimsical music)

138
00:05:17,417 --> 00:05:19,085
Titles by Frederick's
of Hollywood.

139
00:05:20,253 --> 00:05:23,589
Iowa State College, the
high school after high school.

140
00:05:27,460 --> 00:05:30,930
(peaceful music)

141
00:05:31,764 --> 00:05:35,134
Psst! Carol, got any shovies?

142
00:05:35,168 --> 00:05:39,472
I was boring until
I got my toning perm.

143
00:05:39,505 --> 00:05:43,276
If I had joined the FFA
my life would be complete.

144
00:05:43,309 --> 00:05:45,244
I'd till the soil,
I'd bail some hay,

145
00:05:45,278 --> 00:05:46,579
- and make some--
- Wow, quite a hair-do

146
00:05:46,612 --> 00:05:47,947
on that gal in the middle.

147
00:05:47,980 --> 00:05:50,483
Kay probably can't
remember the day or the date.

148
00:05:50,516 --> 00:05:55,088
All she remembers is that she
was sitting in the study hall.

149
00:05:55,121 --> 00:05:56,422
With a loaded gun.

150
00:05:56,456 --> 00:05:58,424
Alice was going to town
on her physics assignment.

151
00:05:58,458 --> 00:05:59,492
Go Alice, go!

152
00:05:59,525 --> 00:06:00,460
And Frank...

153
00:06:00,493 --> 00:06:03,763
Well, he was busy with
his regular routine.

154
00:06:03,796 --> 00:06:04,931
Mm-hmm.

155
00:06:04,964 --> 00:06:07,767
It was just the
usual sort of day.

156
00:06:07,800 --> 00:06:09,268
I think I'll get a tattoo.

157
00:06:09,302 --> 00:06:10,336
Or was it?

158
00:06:10,370 --> 00:06:12,605
She was eating cubes
of mescaline Jello.

159
00:06:12,638 --> 00:06:13,539
There's the bell
for an assembly.

160
00:06:14,974 --> 00:06:18,177
Like Pavlov's dogs they
salivate at the sound of a bell.

161
00:06:21,647 --> 00:06:23,616
This one was
for the girls only.

162
00:06:23,649 --> 00:06:24,684
- Ohh...
- Oh!

163
00:06:24,717 --> 00:06:26,619
Nothing too
unusual about that.

164
00:06:26,652 --> 00:06:28,287
The Chippendales
were in town.

165
00:06:28,321 --> 00:06:29,355
Kay found a seat.

166
00:06:29,389 --> 00:06:32,291
Her favorite, right
next to the aisle.

167
00:06:32,325 --> 00:06:34,494
In case she barfs.
She's got a tender tummy.

168
00:06:34,527 --> 00:06:36,396
Whoa, that one.

169
00:06:38,664 --> 00:06:41,334
Your period and
mine, a lecture.

170
00:06:44,203 --> 00:06:45,171
Hmm?

171
00:06:45,405 --> 00:06:46,706
Hmm?

172
00:06:46,739 --> 00:06:49,542
Hello, am I on?
Is this thing on?

173
00:06:49,575 --> 00:06:50,710
You wanna look at that, Helen?

174
00:06:52,178 --> 00:06:54,213
Today, I'd like to tell you

175
00:06:54,247 --> 00:06:56,649
about several girls
I know very well.

176
00:06:56,682 --> 00:06:58,151
And why I'm being fired.

177
00:06:58,184 --> 00:07:00,319
I took several heavy blows to
the cheeks with a lead pipe.

178
00:07:00,353 --> 00:07:02,054
Were wondering,
just as you are,

179
00:07:02,088 --> 00:07:05,525
what they would do after
completing high school.

180
00:07:05,558 --> 00:07:07,193
Let's start with Eveline,

181
00:07:07,226 --> 00:07:08,361
who went on to college,

182
00:07:08,394 --> 00:07:11,097
where she took a course
in home economics.

183
00:07:12,231 --> 00:07:13,733
Now she's a
tea room manager--

184
00:07:13,766 --> 00:07:14,867
Hey her voice changed.

185
00:07:14,901 --> 00:07:16,536
Thousands of
people who eat out,

186
00:07:16,569 --> 00:07:18,070
are her daily customers.

187
00:07:18,104 --> 00:07:20,173
You might see her
talking to the hostess

188
00:07:20,206 --> 00:07:22,341
and never guess she's
really the boss.

189
00:07:22,375 --> 00:07:24,010
Shut up, Grandma,
you're lucky to have a job.

190
00:07:24,043 --> 00:07:25,244
Behind the scene,

191
00:07:25,278 --> 00:07:27,580
she's the one who
makes the final check.

192
00:07:27,613 --> 00:07:29,715
And adds that extra touch.

193
00:07:29,749 --> 00:07:30,850
Of Salmonella.

194
00:07:30,883 --> 00:07:32,351
Ooh, waffles.

195
00:07:32,385 --> 00:07:34,520
Kay thought she would
like that sort of work.

196
00:07:34,554 --> 00:07:35,555
Kay was wrong.

197
00:07:35,588 --> 00:07:37,356
She could see herself
at the center of a world,

198
00:07:37,390 --> 00:07:39,192
made up of large
ovens and mixers--

199
00:07:39,225 --> 00:07:40,359
And large
white-haired ladies.

200
00:07:40,393 --> 00:07:42,195
Manned by professional cooks.

201
00:07:42,228 --> 00:07:45,531
Gee, what would Mom think
of a grocery list like this?

202
00:07:45,565 --> 00:07:46,699
She'd laugh.

203
00:07:46,732 --> 00:07:48,401
For any woman training in
institution management though,

204
00:07:48,434 --> 00:07:50,436
it's just like planning
the family dinner.

205
00:07:50,470 --> 00:07:52,405
That's right I said
a full case of vodka.

206
00:07:54,140 --> 00:07:56,375
Then one day Kay fiddled
with accounts payable,

207
00:07:56,409 --> 00:07:57,877
took a long drive
into the country

208
00:07:57,910 --> 00:07:59,545
and got cabin A,
at the Bates Motel.

209
00:07:59,579 --> 00:08:00,813
And this is Joanne.

210
00:08:00,847 --> 00:08:03,549
In high school, she always
helped decorate for the parties.

211
00:08:03,583 --> 00:08:04,750
She liked it.

212
00:08:04,784 --> 00:08:07,253
- She still decorates.
- For Bozo.

213
00:08:07,286 --> 00:08:09,055
Gets good pay
now for doing it.

214
00:08:09,088 --> 00:08:11,057
An applied art major
in home economics

215
00:08:11,090 --> 00:08:13,059
helped her develop this ability.

216
00:08:13,092 --> 00:08:15,294
Kay couldn't resist daydreaming

217
00:08:15,328 --> 00:08:17,396
herself into this kind of job.

218
00:08:17,430 --> 00:08:21,300
Folks would look to her to
help them make a wise choice.

219
00:08:21,334 --> 00:08:24,237
She would be asked to
go out on appointments,

220
00:08:24,270 --> 00:08:27,573
to help people plan
lovelier homes.

221
00:08:27,607 --> 00:08:31,110
Ah, the swatches for
the ponchos I ordered.

222
00:08:32,445 --> 00:08:34,580
The speaker told of
other girls she knew.

223
00:08:34,614 --> 00:08:36,949
- I'm huge.
- Real girls, in real jobs.

224
00:08:36,983 --> 00:08:39,085
Good jobs. Interesting jobs.

225
00:08:39,118 --> 00:08:40,419
Well paid, too.

226
00:08:40,453 --> 00:08:43,456
And every one had taken her
training in home economics.

227
00:08:43,489 --> 00:08:44,590
Including Chad.

228
00:08:44,624 --> 00:08:46,492
Some had specialized
in foods and nutrition.

229
00:08:46,526 --> 00:08:50,329
Others in education,
and clothing, also--

230
00:08:50,363 --> 00:08:52,265
- Gee, slow down!
- Child development--

231
00:08:52,298 --> 00:08:53,432
- God!
- Rock our film...

232
00:08:53,466 --> 00:08:56,102
Applied art,
institution management,

233
00:08:56,135 --> 00:08:58,104
- Save us.
- Journalism.

234
00:08:58,137 --> 00:09:01,674
Home economics is all
of this and more, too.

235
00:09:01,707 --> 00:09:02,608
Damn.

236
00:09:02,642 --> 00:09:03,809
For instance...

237
00:09:03,843 --> 00:09:06,445
Carol, who is now
Mrs. Bill Johnson,

238
00:09:06,479 --> 00:09:08,447
took a general home
economics course.

239
00:09:08,481 --> 00:09:10,516
I don't want my wife working.
Get me a beer. (burping)

240
00:09:10,550 --> 00:09:13,352
Professional employment but,
one which fitted her,

241
00:09:13,386 --> 00:09:17,623
for that very important
career of being Mrs. Johnson.

242
00:09:17,657 --> 00:09:19,492
(booing)

243
00:09:20,826 --> 00:09:23,129
Kay's home would be like this.

244
00:09:23,162 --> 00:09:24,463
A dungeon.

245
00:09:24,497 --> 00:09:25,498
Mmm.

246
00:09:28,167 --> 00:09:29,335
Now what the hell is that?

247
00:09:29,368 --> 00:09:31,137
She was glad that
her college training

248
00:09:31,170 --> 00:09:33,306
had prepared her so
well for home life.

249
00:09:33,339 --> 00:09:35,741
How much did that cost?
Well, bring it back.

250
00:09:38,744 --> 00:09:40,513
You must let me dress you.

251
00:09:43,015 --> 00:09:44,183
Can I have a feel too?

252
00:09:44,216 --> 00:09:45,651
Many times, she
didn't even realize

253
00:09:45,685 --> 00:09:48,387
that she was using some of the
knowledge that she'd gained.

254
00:09:48,421 --> 00:09:51,691
And that her knowledge
meant so much to her family.

255
00:09:51,724 --> 00:09:54,327
That's her husband
Jerry, with his new car.

256
00:09:54,360 --> 00:09:56,429
(whispering) Escape. Escape.

257
00:09:58,364 --> 00:10:01,500
It wasn't hard for
Alice to return to reality.

258
00:10:01,534 --> 00:10:04,170
- She just went cold-turkey.
- But, as for Kay,

259
00:10:04,203 --> 00:10:06,005
- Kay, psst!
- Well...

260
00:10:06,038 --> 00:10:07,340
Making a dream come true,

261
00:10:07,373 --> 00:10:10,676
often depends on a
very little thing.

262
00:10:10,710 --> 00:10:12,378
Window pane.

263
00:10:13,946 --> 00:10:15,381
Hmm, let's see.

264
00:10:15,414 --> 00:10:17,350
Day after day she had--

265
00:10:17,383 --> 00:10:19,018
Hmm, Our Bodies Ourselves,

266
00:10:19,051 --> 00:10:20,353
The Bell Jar, oh here it is.

267
00:10:20,386 --> 00:10:22,388
Ghetto Freaks, there it is.

268
00:10:22,421 --> 00:10:25,024
Of course, there were
things she would have to do.

269
00:10:25,057 --> 00:10:28,761
Apply for admission,
study the courses and...

270
00:10:28,794 --> 00:10:30,229
- Drinking.
- Oh, yes.

271
00:10:30,262 --> 00:10:32,565
What would Mom and Dad say?

272
00:10:32,598 --> 00:10:34,200
Who knows, they
only speak German.

273
00:10:34,233 --> 00:10:37,036
There was only one way
to find out, ask them.

274
00:10:37,069 --> 00:10:38,371
This was important.

275
00:10:38,404 --> 00:10:41,374
She realized that now, even
before they said anything.

276
00:10:41,407 --> 00:10:43,409
- She's loaded.
- You could tell that Mom

277
00:10:43,442 --> 00:10:45,711
and Dad knew it
was important too.

278
00:10:45,745 --> 00:10:48,381
College for Kay
would mean sacrifices

279
00:10:48,414 --> 00:10:50,216
- for Mom and Dad.
- Human sacrifices.

280
00:10:50,249 --> 00:10:52,551
Would it be
worth the sacrifice?

281
00:10:52,585 --> 00:10:56,389
(peaceful music)

282
00:10:56,422 --> 00:10:59,225
She consulted
Robert McNamara.

283
00:10:59,258 --> 00:11:01,560
And Ayn Rand.

284
00:11:01,594 --> 00:11:02,928
Do it, sis!

285
00:11:07,667 --> 00:11:10,469
Dear Dean Johnson, I
couldn't really begin to--

286
00:11:10,503 --> 00:11:13,406
So, this was the
important day in Kay's life.

287
00:11:13,439 --> 00:11:14,740
And it was late at night,

288
00:11:14,774 --> 00:11:16,308
when she finally recopied the
letter, for the last time.

289
00:11:16,342 --> 00:11:18,077
I quiver at the
touch of your--

290
00:11:18,110 --> 00:11:20,913
She got a real thrill out of
dropping that letter in the box.

291
00:11:20,946 --> 00:11:21,947
(laughing)

292
00:11:21,981 --> 00:11:22,915
Be cool. Be cool.
(laughing)

293
00:11:22,948 --> 00:11:24,083
Come on, be cool, just...

294
00:11:24,116 --> 00:11:26,318
Oh, that's good. Now
let's get out of here.

295
00:11:26,352 --> 00:11:27,420
It's here already!

296
00:11:27,453 --> 00:11:29,455
Oh, shoot! I mailed
it to myself.

297
00:11:31,290 --> 00:11:33,292
Waiting for a
reply was harder.

298
00:11:33,325 --> 00:11:35,261
It's from Ed McMahon.

299
00:11:35,294 --> 00:11:37,096
It says I already
may be a winner!

300
00:11:37,129 --> 00:11:37,797
Yay!

301
00:11:39,131 --> 00:11:42,268
She had months to go,
but she just couldn't wait

302
00:11:42,301 --> 00:11:43,936
to get out of that
stifling home-life.

303
00:11:43,969 --> 00:11:46,672
(exciting music)

304
00:11:46,706 --> 00:11:49,542
Dear, why are you packing
your father's things?

305
00:11:51,143 --> 00:11:52,478
So, this is college.

306
00:11:52,511 --> 00:11:54,113
So, this is Pod City Vo-Tech.

307
00:11:54,146 --> 00:11:55,347
Will have a homey look.

308
00:11:55,381 --> 00:11:57,450
Now it was a bit bewildering.

309
00:11:57,483 --> 00:11:59,285
Wonder where the
tennis courts are.

310
00:11:59,318 --> 00:12:01,187
Where would her room be?

311
00:12:01,220 --> 00:12:03,289
Who would be her friends?

312
00:12:03,322 --> 00:12:04,490
Who would be
her blood-enemies?

313
00:12:04,523 --> 00:12:06,459
Wonder if she'd ever
know her way around here.

314
00:12:06,492 --> 00:12:08,127
What secret societies
would she join?

315
00:12:08,160 --> 00:12:10,029
Would she smoke
thin black cigarettes

316
00:12:10,062 --> 00:12:11,497
and reject The Triune God?

317
00:12:12,998 --> 00:12:14,166
Well, here's the dorm-mother,

318
00:12:14,200 --> 00:12:15,968
affectionately
known as Horse Face.

319
00:12:16,001 --> 00:12:17,136
(horse sounds)

320
00:12:17,169 --> 00:12:18,304
You lost the draw.

321
00:12:18,337 --> 00:12:19,672
You'll be rooming
with the Ice Queen.

322
00:12:19,705 --> 00:12:22,842
At first it looks bare
and not very friendly.

323
00:12:22,875 --> 00:12:24,810
Who? Her roommate?

324
00:12:24,844 --> 00:12:26,712
Hmm, smells like
high-grade weed in here.

325
00:12:26,746 --> 00:12:28,814
We'll make a loft out of some
railroad ties, under the bed,

326
00:12:28,848 --> 00:12:30,816
a Special Export light
in the window, and--

327
00:12:30,850 --> 00:12:33,319
But with new
curtains and bedspread,

328
00:12:33,352 --> 00:12:35,888
it doesn't take long to
give it a homey feeling,

329
00:12:35,921 --> 00:12:37,323
and a bit of college atmosphere.

330
00:12:37,356 --> 00:12:39,191
I've got a feeling
Mom majored in

331
00:12:39,225 --> 00:12:41,160
home economics at Iowa State.

332
00:12:41,193 --> 00:12:42,495
(humming)

333
00:12:42,528 --> 00:12:45,197
Before you know it, it's
time for the folks to leave.

334
00:12:45,231 --> 00:12:47,199
In all her dreams about college,

335
00:12:47,233 --> 00:12:49,001
Kay had never
included the moment

336
00:12:49,034 --> 00:12:51,504
when Dad and Mom said goodbye,

337
00:12:51,537 --> 00:12:54,373
when she would be on her own.

338
00:12:54,406 --> 00:12:56,008
See you in four
years. Don't call.

339
00:12:56,041 --> 00:12:57,510
A friend always, love Dad.

340
00:12:57,543 --> 00:13:00,579
Thank god there was
no physical contact.

341
00:13:00,613 --> 00:13:01,413
Mm-hmm.

342
00:13:01,447 --> 00:13:05,050
Mom, Dad, no, no.

343
00:13:05,084 --> 00:13:07,386
Well, just leave then!

344
00:13:07,419 --> 00:13:10,189
Forget you! I never loved you!

345
00:13:10,222 --> 00:13:12,358
(blubbering)

346
00:13:12,391 --> 00:13:13,592
Poor kid.

347
00:13:16,962 --> 00:13:18,531
Now she'll race down to the

348
00:13:18,564 --> 00:13:21,901
Jean-Luc Godard festival
at the campus theater.

349
00:13:23,402 --> 00:13:26,572
(singing) I'm so alone,
I don't know what to do.

350
00:13:26,605 --> 00:13:30,376
I look at you and
I go out of focus.

351
00:13:30,409 --> 00:13:31,877
Why does she have a picture of

352
00:13:31,911 --> 00:13:33,445
John Carradine on
her nightstand?

353
00:13:33,479 --> 00:13:35,948
(whispering) That's
Mom. That's Mom.

354
00:13:37,917 --> 00:13:40,953
Oh, will you ever
accept my Marxist ways?

355
00:13:40,986 --> 00:13:42,421
Arrived just when she did.

356
00:13:42,454 --> 00:13:43,956
Oh, hi, how you doing?

357
00:13:43,989 --> 00:13:45,391
We're gonna have a great time.

358
00:13:45,424 --> 00:13:46,892
We're gonna be pals!

359
00:13:46,926 --> 00:13:48,561
The usual things.

360
00:13:48,594 --> 00:13:49,795
And while they were talking...

361
00:13:49,829 --> 00:13:53,299
Two of the ripest
tomatoes in town strolled in.

362
00:13:53,332 --> 00:13:55,501
Hi, you got a bong?

363
00:13:56,602 --> 00:14:00,906
Four girls, Kay and
Jean, Helen and Louise.

364
00:14:00,940 --> 00:14:03,142
- The group.
- For the next four years,

365
00:14:03,175 --> 00:14:04,410
they will see a
lot of each other.

366
00:14:04,443 --> 00:14:05,811
(chuckling)
Come on, they just met.

367
00:14:05,845 --> 00:14:09,448
Most of their experiences
will be shared with each other.

368
00:14:09,481 --> 00:14:11,617
Kegs will be tapped.
Men will be used.

369
00:14:12,685 --> 00:14:14,587
They're strangely drawn
to this mystic fountain.

370
00:14:14,620 --> 00:14:16,622
The first stroll, with
its first impressions.

371
00:14:16,655 --> 00:14:19,491
Thank goodness for
Head and Shoulders.

372
00:14:21,327 --> 00:14:23,462
They stopped at
the same places.

373
00:14:23,495 --> 00:14:25,164
Three coins in a fountain.

374
00:14:25,197 --> 00:14:26,799
They looked at
the same scenes.

375
00:14:27,967 --> 00:14:30,936
They did the same things
that many had done before.

376
00:14:30,970 --> 00:14:32,137
There was sameness.

377
00:14:32,171 --> 00:14:35,674
College, with its new
friendships, its old traditions.

378
00:14:35,708 --> 00:14:37,776
- (snoring)
- By the end of freshman week,

379
00:14:37,810 --> 00:14:40,279
they had begun to feel that
this college was their very own.

380
00:14:40,312 --> 00:14:43,148
They started by taking over
the administration building.

381
00:14:43,182 --> 00:14:44,783
Off the pigs. Off the pigs.

382
00:14:44,817 --> 00:14:48,654
How do you solve a
problem like a college girl.

383
00:14:50,823 --> 00:14:52,124
Early October,

384
00:14:52,157 --> 00:14:54,860
headed for an eight o'clock
class in home-ec hall.

385
00:14:54,894 --> 00:14:55,828
Tragedy struck.

386
00:14:55,861 --> 00:14:57,663
A lone gunman...
(mock gun sounds)

387
00:14:58,497 --> 00:15:00,165
Here's the
young Judy Chicago.

388
00:15:01,500 --> 00:15:03,168
It helped to build up a
knowledge of the beauty--

389
00:15:03,202 --> 00:15:04,870
Don't sit down! (laughing)

390
00:15:04,904 --> 00:15:07,139
Surprising, how
everyone can make such

391
00:15:07,172 --> 00:15:08,807
interesting, original designs.

392
00:15:08,841 --> 00:15:11,310
While Kay struggles
with basic motor-skills.

393
00:15:11,343 --> 00:15:12,845
Ooh.

394
00:15:14,179 --> 00:15:17,650
And this study of design
and color, wasn't all theory.

395
00:15:17,683 --> 00:15:20,653
Jean and Helen learned to
apply it in their class,

396
00:15:20,686 --> 00:15:22,988
on costume design and selection.

397
00:15:23,022 --> 00:15:25,324
Is that a real poncho
or a Sears poncho?

398
00:15:25,357 --> 00:15:26,492
Watch those Huskies.

399
00:15:26,525 --> 00:15:28,327
All the classes
seemed to fit together.

400
00:15:28,360 --> 00:15:29,662
I love this one, watch this.

401
00:15:29,695 --> 00:15:31,497
Even the physics class
was what a girl would like.

402
00:15:31,530 --> 00:15:32,831
Would you come in here,
I need you. (laughing)

403
00:15:32,865 --> 00:15:35,334
It was about
physics in the home.

404
00:15:35,367 --> 00:15:38,337
This course helps
you to understand

405
00:15:38,370 --> 00:15:40,072
just what makes things run.

406
00:15:41,206 --> 00:15:42,408
Then in household equipment,

407
00:15:42,441 --> 00:15:44,343
you could study
the practical side.

408
00:15:44,376 --> 00:15:45,511
The use of an appliance...

409
00:15:45,544 --> 00:15:47,212
Being treated
like an appliance.

410
00:15:47,246 --> 00:15:50,516
In turn there were courses
in foods and nutrition,

411
00:15:50,549 --> 00:15:52,351
where you also learn the why...

412
00:15:52,384 --> 00:15:54,353
- This is phlegm.
- As well as the how.

413
00:15:54,386 --> 00:15:55,521
This is mucus.

414
00:15:55,554 --> 00:15:59,758
(Mystery Science Theater
3000 theme music)

415
00:16:02,394 --> 00:16:04,363
Hey, it's the
Woodstock of the 50's.

416
00:16:04,396 --> 00:16:05,531
Vic Damone's on next.

417
00:16:05,564 --> 00:16:07,366
Play Whipping Post! Woo!

418
00:16:07,399 --> 00:16:10,369
Saturday afternoon,
football time.

419
00:16:10,402 --> 00:16:12,071
I'm a Q-tip. What are you?

420
00:16:14,139 --> 00:16:16,542
You were there with
all the spirit you had,

421
00:16:16,575 --> 00:16:18,610
for your high school team
and maybe a little more.

422
00:16:18,644 --> 00:16:19,878
Look! Look!
Look at my crotch!

423
00:16:19,912 --> 00:16:22,247
Look at my crotch!
Look at my crotch!

424
00:16:22,281 --> 00:16:24,783
Look! Look! Look at my crotch!

425
00:16:24,817 --> 00:16:28,053
Looooooook! Look at my crotch!

426
00:16:28,087 --> 00:16:29,922
Yay!

427
00:16:32,424 --> 00:16:35,094
And afterwards,
you met the gang.

428
00:16:35,127 --> 00:16:38,063
And you really got slammed,
then apologized all day Monday.

429
00:16:38,097 --> 00:16:39,565
Hey there's Neal Cassady.

430
00:16:39,598 --> 00:16:40,733
I didn't know he
went to college.

431
00:16:40,766 --> 00:16:42,968
- Only for chicks.
- Oh.

432
00:16:43,002 --> 00:16:44,103
Hiya, Tank.

433
00:16:48,173 --> 00:16:50,476
Hats off to Ray, the
whimsical lampshade.

434
00:16:50,509 --> 00:16:52,578
After such an occasion,

435
00:16:52,611 --> 00:16:55,080
the girls got together for
their usual gab session.

436
00:16:55,114 --> 00:16:58,083
For a time, they talked
of the events of the day.

437
00:16:58,117 --> 00:17:01,754
But then, Kay came up with
that all-important question.

438
00:17:01,787 --> 00:17:03,589
How do Pop-Tarts work?

439
00:17:03,622 --> 00:17:05,424
What are you going
to major in, Helen?

440
00:17:05,457 --> 00:17:06,658
Oh, you know me.

441
00:17:06,692 --> 00:17:08,827
Best cook to come
out of Foods 204.

442
00:17:08,861 --> 00:17:12,264
I'm going to take
Foods and Nutrition.

443
00:17:12,297 --> 00:17:13,599
Really, I am.

444
00:17:13,632 --> 00:17:14,433
I'm gonna marry
a lighthouse keeper.

445
00:17:14,466 --> 00:17:15,467
I want to be a dietitian.

446
00:17:15,501 --> 00:17:16,435
Right, you're
gonna be a short-order

447
00:17:16,468 --> 00:17:17,636
cook for truckers in two years.

448
00:17:17,669 --> 00:17:19,772
Wish I could make
up my mind that easy.

449
00:17:19,805 --> 00:17:21,140
What are you gonna take, Jean?

450
00:17:21,173 --> 00:17:23,275
I'm gonna take Bob
for everything he's got.

451
00:17:23,308 --> 00:17:26,612
I like to work with
fabrics, beautiful clothes--

452
00:17:26,645 --> 00:17:28,514
Hey look, it's
Lou Costello in drag.

453
00:17:28,547 --> 00:17:29,982
Department stores...

454
00:17:31,316 --> 00:17:32,684
I'd like to go
along with you.

455
00:17:32,718 --> 00:17:35,454
But I don't know yet
whether I want to

456
00:17:35,487 --> 00:17:37,790
work for a household
appliance company,

457
00:17:37,823 --> 00:17:40,192
or whether to go
on with my artwork.

458
00:17:40,225 --> 00:17:42,294
Oh, these are the kind of
problems you want to have.

459
00:17:42,327 --> 00:17:44,129
Do you know what
I'm going to do?

460
00:17:44,163 --> 00:17:45,497
I'm going to teach.

461
00:17:45,531 --> 00:17:46,465
Cause I can't do.

462
00:17:46,498 --> 00:17:48,167
Kay went to chat
with her adviser.

463
00:17:48,200 --> 00:17:49,868
- She learned about--
- You wanna what?

464
00:17:49,902 --> 00:17:53,172
Opportunities, things
she'd never thought of before.

465
00:17:53,205 --> 00:17:55,474
Listen to the flowers.

466
00:17:55,507 --> 00:17:56,809
They will tell
you sweet secrets.

467
00:17:56,842 --> 00:17:58,310
High school teaching,

468
00:17:58,343 --> 00:18:01,680
nursery school work, for those
who like very small children.

469
00:18:01,713 --> 00:18:03,682
And for medium sized
kids, go to a middle school.

470
00:18:03,715 --> 00:18:05,684
And teaching farm families.

471
00:18:06,418 --> 00:18:07,986
Would teaching be her career?

472
00:18:08,020 --> 00:18:09,688
Or would it be the
business world,

473
00:18:09,721 --> 00:18:11,557
with its glamorous
commercial jobs?

474
00:18:11,590 --> 00:18:12,891
And low-paying temp jobs.

475
00:18:12,925 --> 00:18:14,693
Would it be
public service field?

476
00:18:14,726 --> 00:18:16,995
Perhaps a hospital dietitian?

477
00:18:17,029 --> 00:18:19,331
Or research, with its
look to the future?

478
00:18:19,364 --> 00:18:22,835
Yes, there would be all
sorts of interesting careers,

479
00:18:22,868 --> 00:18:24,503
- and best of all--
- Hey look, it's Prince!

480
00:18:24,536 --> 00:18:26,038
(laughing) That's
his royal badness,

481
00:18:26,071 --> 00:18:28,574
when he was still a
chubby little white kid.

482
00:18:28,607 --> 00:18:31,210
See him up there, in purple?

483
00:18:31,243 --> 00:18:34,513
- Oh, mm-hmm that's a reach.
- I'm sorry.

484
00:18:34,546 --> 00:18:37,683
College is a timeless period.

485
00:18:37,716 --> 00:18:40,519
One year passes into
another, very quickly.

486
00:18:40,552 --> 00:18:42,054
- Soon you die.
- It's good to be back.

487
00:18:42,087 --> 00:18:43,856
At the beginning of
your junior year,

488
00:18:43,889 --> 00:18:45,891
things seem pretty
much the same.

489
00:18:45,924 --> 00:18:47,192
But this is the year--

490
00:18:47,226 --> 00:18:49,361
The National
Guardsman were on campus.

491
00:18:49,394 --> 00:18:50,729
In your chosen field.

492
00:18:52,231 --> 00:18:54,867
Helen, learning to be
a hospital dietitian,

493
00:18:54,900 --> 00:18:57,369
must be able to plan and
serve inviting meals.

494
00:18:57,402 --> 00:18:59,705
Plenty of butter, pile
it on, good hospital food.

495
00:18:59,738 --> 00:19:01,573
Plan well enough to
fit a doctors orders,

496
00:19:01,607 --> 00:19:03,041
and serve them attractively--

497
00:19:03,075 --> 00:19:05,277
Oh, please don't
eat the daisies.

498
00:19:05,310 --> 00:19:06,879
Mmm, square eggs.

499
00:19:06,912 --> 00:19:08,046
She'll also have the problem

500
00:19:08,080 --> 00:19:09,715
of feeding large
numbers of people.

501
00:19:09,748 --> 00:19:10,883
And her fat brother-in-law.

502
00:19:10,916 --> 00:19:12,217
That calls for
quantity cookery,

503
00:19:12,251 --> 00:19:15,554
so you'll find her in classes
in institution management.

504
00:19:15,587 --> 00:19:17,723
For quality cookery,
use the Makery Bakery.

505
00:19:17,756 --> 00:19:20,058
Louise, who has her
eye on a commercial

506
00:19:20,092 --> 00:19:21,894
career in household equipment,

507
00:19:21,927 --> 00:19:24,563
is rapidly becoming a
technician in her own field.

508
00:19:24,596 --> 00:19:25,898
Wiring bombs.

509
00:19:25,931 --> 00:19:28,934
The technical comparisons
she is making now--

510
00:19:28,967 --> 00:19:30,402
Are completely off-base.

511
00:19:30,435 --> 00:19:33,338
Firsthand advice on the
selection of home appliances.

512
00:19:36,275 --> 00:19:39,444
Her knowledge of efficient
operation will be passed along--

513
00:19:39,478 --> 00:19:40,579
She could never
be an engineer.

514
00:19:40,612 --> 00:19:43,248
Don't make me laugh. Ha!

515
00:19:43,282 --> 00:19:43,482
Now cough.

516
00:19:44,783 --> 00:19:47,586
Jean was just as busy
in textiles and clothing.

517
00:19:47,619 --> 00:19:48,921
Ah, very attractive awning.

518
00:19:48,954 --> 00:19:51,790
The world of fabrics and
of fashions will be hers.

519
00:19:51,823 --> 00:19:54,493
Here, she designs pants
for Chuckles the clown.

520
00:19:56,795 --> 00:19:59,598
Perhaps she'll be
designing the clothing

521
00:19:59,631 --> 00:20:01,833
you see pictured in
fashion magazines.

522
00:20:01,867 --> 00:20:03,101
Oh, I don't know.

523
00:20:03,135 --> 00:20:04,336
Perhaps she'll
be the person who

524
00:20:04,369 --> 00:20:07,272
thought up the patterns
you will buy at the store.

525
00:20:07,306 --> 00:20:08,473
It's possible.

526
00:20:08,507 --> 00:20:10,108
Perhaps she will
show you how you can

527
00:20:10,142 --> 00:20:12,611
create beautiful
clothes for yourself.

528
00:20:12,644 --> 00:20:14,513
Or help you chose
them at the store.

529
00:20:14,546 --> 00:20:16,181
(humming)

530
00:20:16,215 --> 00:20:17,349
Oh, hi Opal.

531
00:20:17,382 --> 00:20:20,185
Jean may even find
another use for her talents.

532
00:20:20,219 --> 00:20:23,455
By combining her knowledge
of art and textiles--

533
00:20:23,488 --> 00:20:24,856
She makes tar-heroin.

534
00:20:24,890 --> 00:20:26,291
She may follow a career
in textile designing.

535
00:20:26,325 --> 00:20:28,694
Let's face it, Jean would
do anything for a buck.

536
00:20:28,727 --> 00:20:30,195
Mm-hmm.

537
00:20:30,229 --> 00:20:32,464
The days are filled with
other interesting classes too.

538
00:20:32,497 --> 00:20:33,966
You would expect to find Kay,

539
00:20:33,999 --> 00:20:35,300
with her interest in teaching--

540
00:20:35,334 --> 00:20:37,336
- I'm getting light-headed.
- Keep going!

541
00:20:37,369 --> 00:20:38,870
But Jean is there too.

542
00:20:38,904 --> 00:20:41,673
To study children's
clothing requirements?

543
00:20:41,707 --> 00:20:45,310
Well, yes, but more than that.

544
00:20:45,344 --> 00:20:47,212
Home economics means
much more than--

545
00:20:47,246 --> 00:20:49,381
What? We have to
be subjugated to men?

546
00:20:49,414 --> 00:20:52,484
The study of everything
that makes up a home,

547
00:20:52,517 --> 00:20:54,820
including those who live there.

548
00:20:54,853 --> 00:20:56,521
The girls learn a lot
from the children--

549
00:20:56,555 --> 00:20:58,190
Are the kids
learning anything?

550
00:20:58,223 --> 00:21:00,659
All habits and attitudes
learned at this age,

551
00:21:00,692 --> 00:21:02,160
are so important in later life.

552
00:21:02,194 --> 00:21:04,162
It's too tight!
It's too tight!

553
00:21:04,196 --> 00:21:06,698
It's calamari,
mmm. (smacking lips)

554
00:21:06,732 --> 00:21:08,233
After all, we
live in a world where

555
00:21:08,267 --> 00:21:11,336
people are more
important than things.

556
00:21:11,370 --> 00:21:12,537
Oh, I don't know.

557
00:21:12,571 --> 00:21:15,874
(peaceful music)

558
00:21:20,379 --> 00:21:22,414
Yes, college is a rich--

559
00:21:22,447 --> 00:21:23,682
It says piggy on the wall.

560
00:21:23,715 --> 00:21:25,050
- Where?
- Over there.

561
00:21:25,083 --> 00:21:27,586
You tell Mom and Dad
about the little events.

562
00:21:27,619 --> 00:21:30,422
One little boy
was just darling.

563
00:21:30,455 --> 00:21:32,024
I really like that class.

564
00:21:32,057 --> 00:21:33,859
And keep the important
stuff to yourself.

565
00:21:33,892 --> 00:21:35,761
- And the big ones, too.
- Oh.

566
00:21:35,794 --> 00:21:37,429
Tonight is the big prom.

567
00:21:37,462 --> 00:21:39,698
- I'm wearing my new--
- Everyone will die.

568
00:21:39,731 --> 00:21:42,067
I will dump pig's
blood on Carrie.

569
00:21:43,068 --> 00:21:44,870
You know, she's
gonna look like

570
00:21:44,903 --> 00:21:47,539
an unmade bed, in that dress.

571
00:21:47,572 --> 00:21:49,374
Each good time that you had,

572
00:21:49,408 --> 00:21:52,744
mingled with the memories
of other good times.

573
00:21:52,778 --> 00:21:54,713
And makes a delicous gravy.

574
00:21:54,746 --> 00:21:56,281
You'll never forget them.

575
00:21:56,315 --> 00:21:58,283
They were the
glittering threads,

576
00:21:58,317 --> 00:22:01,386
woven through the very
pattern of college life.

577
00:22:01,420 --> 00:22:04,489
Here, a little sample
of each of her victims.

578
00:22:06,158 --> 00:22:08,260
That's Bob, and Carl, and Steve.

579
00:22:09,661 --> 00:22:12,931
Being a senior wasn't much
different from being a junior.

580
00:22:12,964 --> 00:22:15,300
Student teaching
was in line for Kay.

581
00:22:15,334 --> 00:22:18,003
But Kay was out of line
with student teaching.

582
00:22:20,272 --> 00:22:21,940
And Jean was
busier than ever,

583
00:22:21,973 --> 00:22:25,077
in her advanced courses
in textils and clothing.

584
00:22:25,110 --> 00:22:26,745
She hasn't left
this room in years.

585
00:22:26,778 --> 00:22:30,782
Helen was deep in studies
on food buymanship, getting her

586
00:22:30,816 --> 00:22:32,584
- money's worth, that is.
- Grocery shopping.

587
00:22:32,617 --> 00:22:35,320
One of the many things
she'll be doing next year--

588
00:22:35,354 --> 00:22:37,589
Let's see 30
grams to an ounce.

589
00:22:37,622 --> 00:22:39,358
Hey, is this raw or refined?

590
00:22:40,792 --> 00:22:41,927
- Sugar.
- And Louise...

591
00:22:41,960 --> 00:22:43,762
Wouldn't you know she'd be in

592
00:22:43,795 --> 00:22:45,630
a household
equipment laboratory.

593
00:22:47,799 --> 00:22:49,301
No, don't give up!

594
00:22:51,303 --> 00:22:55,607
Home economics, starring
Efrem Zimbalist Jr.

595
00:22:55,640 --> 00:22:56,942
What is home economics?

596
00:22:56,975 --> 00:22:58,643
Boy, you'd think they
would've told us by now.

597
00:22:58,677 --> 00:23:02,447
Is it the technical knowledge
of the equipment in a home?

598
00:23:02,481 --> 00:23:03,682
I don't think so.

599
00:23:03,715 --> 00:23:05,650
Or the understanding of how
to create beautiful clothes--

600
00:23:05,684 --> 00:23:08,453
Mmm, no, no, that's not it.

601
00:23:08,487 --> 00:23:11,823
Is it the skill to prepare
an attractive nourishing meal?

602
00:23:11,857 --> 00:23:13,458
- Maybe.
- Or the ability

603
00:23:13,492 --> 00:23:15,460
to teach others
these many things?

604
00:23:15,494 --> 00:23:17,462
Is it for all time,
this feeling of joy?

605
00:23:17,496 --> 00:23:20,632
Home economics is
all these and more too.

606
00:23:20,665 --> 00:23:23,301
To put it another
way, each girl--

607
00:23:23,335 --> 00:23:25,303
- Hey it's Abbey Road.
- Two careers.

608
00:23:25,337 --> 00:23:27,139
Boy, you're gonna
carry that wieght.

609
00:23:27,172 --> 00:23:28,373
One, her chosen specialty.

610
00:23:28,407 --> 00:23:31,042
The other is a
career in homemaking.

611
00:23:31,076 --> 00:23:32,644
(singing) Here we come,

612
00:23:32,677 --> 00:23:34,646
walking down the street.

613
00:23:34,679 --> 00:23:37,649
And so, as a special
part of their training,

614
00:23:37,682 --> 00:23:39,818
the senior girls
actually share--

615
00:23:39,851 --> 00:23:41,553
There are only six seniors?

616
00:23:43,221 --> 00:23:45,490
First of all, the
girls met their adviser,

617
00:23:45,524 --> 00:23:48,026
and decided how they
were going to take turns

618
00:23:48,059 --> 00:23:50,061
at managing all the
household tasks.

619
00:23:50,095 --> 00:23:52,364
Here Carol Bly
explains her principles.

620
00:23:52,397 --> 00:23:55,167
This was no
make-believe situation.

621
00:23:55,200 --> 00:23:56,501
With eight hungry
persons to feed,

622
00:23:56,535 --> 00:23:58,670
Jean would have to
draw on the knowledge

623
00:23:58,703 --> 00:24:02,240
she'd gained in basic courses
in foods and in equipment.

624
00:24:02,274 --> 00:24:04,543
She had to steal from
every grocery store in town,

625
00:24:04,576 --> 00:24:06,211
as part of her hazing.

626
00:24:07,045 --> 00:24:09,347
As for planning
an attractive meal--

627
00:24:09,381 --> 00:24:10,348
Forget it.

628
00:24:10,382 --> 00:24:12,184
That should be
easy for a girl,

629
00:24:12,217 --> 00:24:15,020
with special talent
in color and design.

630
00:24:15,053 --> 00:24:17,055
But no, not for this woman.

631
00:24:18,557 --> 00:24:20,859
Kay too would draw
on her knowledge of

632
00:24:20,892 --> 00:24:22,060
household equipment
and textiles--

633
00:24:22,093 --> 00:24:23,261
K2, the mountain?

634
00:24:23,295 --> 00:24:24,563
To take care of her
turn in the laundry.

635
00:24:24,596 --> 00:24:26,698
Oh no! You put the laundry
in the composting toilet!

636
00:24:26,731 --> 00:24:28,867
(groaning)

637
00:24:28,900 --> 00:24:31,203
Louise, for a few days,

638
00:24:31,236 --> 00:24:35,040
would have the most
envied task in the house.

639
00:24:35,073 --> 00:24:37,876
Sooner or later, everything
and anything you would

640
00:24:37,909 --> 00:24:40,879
learn in your home economics
courses, would prove useful.

641
00:24:40,912 --> 00:24:42,214
(laughing) That's a good one.

642
00:24:42,247 --> 00:24:43,748
And then she said
you're soaking in it.

643
00:24:43,782 --> 00:24:44,883
(laughing)

644
00:24:44,916 --> 00:24:47,052
(peaceful music)

645
00:24:47,085 --> 00:24:49,454
(singing) Let me
call you sweetheart.

646
00:24:49,488 --> 00:24:52,390
Life in the home management
house is very homey.

647
00:24:52,424 --> 00:24:55,093
Somehow you wish
it would never end.

648
00:24:58,096 --> 00:25:00,232
(humming)

649
00:25:00,265 --> 00:25:03,068
And some of the happenings,
you will never forget.

650
00:25:03,101 --> 00:25:04,769
(chuckling)

651
00:25:05,770 --> 00:25:07,138
(whispering)
Don't open the box.

652
00:25:08,273 --> 00:25:10,742
The five pound
party that Beth had--

653
00:25:10,775 --> 00:25:12,110
Five pound potty?

654
00:25:12,143 --> 00:25:13,578
She had a big
breakfast, didn't she?

655
00:25:13,612 --> 00:25:15,347
- I guess so. (laughing)
- My goodness.

656
00:25:17,616 --> 00:25:20,986
(singing) It's a wonderful
world when you're married.

657
00:25:21,019 --> 00:25:24,489
When you have a family.

658
00:25:26,625 --> 00:25:28,426
The days roll by.

659
00:25:28,460 --> 00:25:30,161
And even before school is out,

660
00:25:30,195 --> 00:25:32,264
you have a new topic
of conversation,

661
00:25:32,297 --> 00:25:35,333
getting your first job,
through the placement office.

662
00:25:35,367 --> 00:25:36,835
Meeting with
Helen Gurley Brown.

663
00:25:38,303 --> 00:25:40,272
Yes, Kay, the
teacher placement office

664
00:25:40,305 --> 00:25:42,474
tells me they're getting
requests every day.

665
00:25:45,010 --> 00:25:46,177
Jean, I think this job--

666
00:25:46,211 --> 00:25:47,445
Oh, she's a
quick-change artist.

667
00:25:47,479 --> 00:25:49,114
This will be very
interesting to you.

668
00:25:49,147 --> 00:25:51,116
- Huzzah!
- This letter has just come in,

669
00:25:51,149 --> 00:25:52,284
telling us that
you've been accepted

670
00:25:52,317 --> 00:25:53,451
for your hosipital internship.

671
00:25:53,485 --> 00:25:55,153
You'll be emptying
bed-pans soon.

672
00:25:55,186 --> 00:25:58,690
Yes, Louise would be
happy to accept the position.

673
00:25:58,723 --> 00:26:00,492
- Akiba.
- Hmm?

674
00:26:00,525 --> 00:26:01,826
Graduation...

675
00:26:01,860 --> 00:26:04,129
Hot and saucy, Texas style.

676
00:26:04,162 --> 00:26:06,331
(giggling)

677
00:26:07,332 --> 00:26:09,968
Mom and Dad were
there, of course.

678
00:26:10,001 --> 00:26:11,202
They didn't say much,

679
00:26:11,236 --> 00:26:12,504
but you knew they were pleased--

680
00:26:12,537 --> 00:26:13,872
Estes Kefauver!

681
00:26:13,905 --> 00:26:15,307
They left early.

682
00:26:15,340 --> 00:26:17,842
Some remark about he
sure had earned his diploma.

683
00:26:17,876 --> 00:26:19,477
Dad just doesn't get it.

684
00:26:19,511 --> 00:26:21,346
Jean and Louise
were leaving at once,

685
00:26:21,379 --> 00:26:22,747
for their jobs in the city.

686
00:26:23,848 --> 00:26:26,318
So you all drove down to
the train to see them off.

687
00:26:26,351 --> 00:26:29,254
And to reenact the last
scene from Anna Karenina.

688
00:26:30,388 --> 00:26:32,223
As you stood there
on the platform,

689
00:26:32,257 --> 00:26:34,726
wishing each other the very
best of good fortune...

690
00:26:34,759 --> 00:26:35,827
You know you didn't mean it.

691
00:26:35,860 --> 00:26:37,362
Somehow the talk got
around to that very--

692
00:26:37,395 --> 00:26:39,030
Speaking of wishing
farewell, let's roll.

693
00:26:39,064 --> 00:26:40,665
And then you could
sense that this was

694
00:26:40,699 --> 00:26:42,834
the end of one
chapter in your life--

695
00:26:42,867 --> 00:26:45,337
Chapters the editor
just spiced up a bit.

696
00:26:45,370 --> 00:26:47,706
- I'm coming. I'm coming.
- Better those than waffles.

697
00:26:47,739 --> 00:26:49,674
You wouldn't have missed.

698
00:26:49,708 --> 00:26:52,544
(happy music)

699
00:26:56,881 --> 00:27:01,886
(doors slamming)

700
00:27:04,723 --> 00:27:06,758
Okay, now take it easy.

701
00:27:06,791 --> 00:27:08,026
Whoa, whoa, hold it up.

702
00:27:08,059 --> 00:27:10,195
What I've done was
reprogram the robots

703
00:27:10,228 --> 00:27:12,697
to love waffles as much as I do.

704
00:27:12,731 --> 00:27:13,932
Can you see?

705
00:27:13,965 --> 00:27:15,200
And what we're gonna do

706
00:27:15,233 --> 00:27:17,202
is name some important new uses

707
00:27:17,235 --> 00:27:19,204
for waffles during this segment.

708
00:27:19,237 --> 00:27:20,405
Okay, who's got one?

709
00:27:20,438 --> 00:27:22,707
Easy to read waffles
within large squares,

710
00:27:22,741 --> 00:27:24,409
for the flavor impaired.

711
00:27:24,442 --> 00:27:26,211
That's good, take it easy.

712
00:27:26,244 --> 00:27:27,379
Can I eat them now?

713
00:27:27,412 --> 00:27:28,413
Buckwheat waffles
make an excellent

714
00:27:28,446 --> 00:27:31,383
shammy for cleaning your car.

715
00:27:31,416 --> 00:27:34,085
Dr. Shoals waffle pad?

716
00:27:34,119 --> 00:27:35,253
Yeah, oh yeah.

717
00:27:35,286 --> 00:27:37,455
Oh, okay. Well,
anyway, now careful.

718
00:27:37,489 --> 00:27:39,424
These are just
for demonstration.

719
00:27:39,457 --> 00:27:44,229
It would be too bad if the
robots got too into it.

720
00:27:44,262 --> 00:27:46,631
I've always been a
breakfast man, myself.

721
00:27:46,665 --> 00:27:47,899
You know me.

722
00:27:47,932 --> 00:27:49,567
Crow, we should really be
working on the assignment.

723
00:27:49,601 --> 00:27:52,103
- Yes.
- But these look so good.

724
00:27:52,137 --> 00:27:53,405
What a taste teaser.

725
00:27:53,438 --> 00:27:55,106
What does this
mean Aunt Jemima's

726
00:27:55,140 --> 00:27:57,575
gonna come put a
waffle in Gypsy's hand?

727
00:27:57,609 --> 00:27:58,410
Take it easy.

728
00:27:58,443 --> 00:28:02,580
It would be too
bad if you robots

729
00:28:02,614 --> 00:28:05,950
were unable to
complete your task

730
00:28:05,984 --> 00:28:08,820
because you were too in love

731
00:28:08,853 --> 00:28:14,259
with this big, juicy
stack of yummy nummies.

732
00:28:14,292 --> 00:28:16,494
Crow, Crow, get a grip.

733
00:28:16,528 --> 00:28:18,463
Gypsy, don't you see
what he's trying to do?

734
00:28:18,496 --> 00:28:20,331
Snap out of it, come on!

735
00:28:20,365 --> 00:28:22,000
Remember the waffle ideas.

736
00:28:22,033 --> 00:28:23,768
Waffle dress shield.

737
00:28:23,802 --> 00:28:25,103
Waffle desk organizer.

738
00:28:25,136 --> 00:28:26,471
Come on, work the steps with me!

739
00:28:26,504 --> 00:28:28,940
Hollywood Squares
waffle (unintelligible)

740
00:28:28,973 --> 00:28:31,609
Wally Cox in the secret
square waffle like sweet--

741
00:28:31,643 --> 00:28:35,980
(yelling)

742
00:28:36,014 --> 00:28:38,283
Joel, I tried buddy.

743
00:28:38,316 --> 00:28:40,118
That's okay, Tom.

744
00:28:40,151 --> 00:28:41,453
Can you just give me one?

745
00:28:41,486 --> 00:28:43,154
Okay, acoustic tiles.

746
00:28:43,188 --> 00:28:46,691
- Go ahead, Tom.
- Here comes Tom Servo!

747
00:28:46,725 --> 00:28:51,463
How about a Greek
waffle called the falafel.

748
00:28:51,496 --> 00:28:56,534
(Mystery Science Theater
3000 theme music)

749
00:28:59,370 --> 00:29:00,705
(chattering)

750
00:29:00,739 --> 00:29:02,173
Put me down.

751
00:29:02,207 --> 00:29:03,508
I was just trying to
get the syrup off you.

752
00:29:03,541 --> 00:29:05,710
Hey look, All My Vikings.

753
00:29:05,744 --> 00:29:08,346
Hey, that's my old
high school yearbook.

754
00:29:08,379 --> 00:29:10,682
Eric's small cigars.

755
00:29:10,715 --> 00:29:11,683
You know what?

756
00:29:11,716 --> 00:29:12,817
People who have
leather-bound books,

757
00:29:12,851 --> 00:29:13,752
they never read them.

758
00:29:14,519 --> 00:29:16,354
The Saga of the Viking Women

759
00:29:16,387 --> 00:29:18,490
and Their Voyage to the Waters
of the Great Sea Serpent

760
00:29:18,523 --> 00:29:21,326
is brought to you by a grant
from the Mobile corporation.

761
00:29:21,359 --> 00:29:23,695
And Dalton Trumbo
as Eric the red.

762
00:29:23,728 --> 00:29:25,563
Dalton? Oh.

763
00:29:27,198 --> 00:29:29,367
With the June Kenney dancers.

764
00:29:30,368 --> 00:29:32,170
Plus Wilfrid Hyde-White,

765
00:29:32,203 --> 00:29:34,339
as the uncredited
cameo appearance.

766
00:29:34,372 --> 00:29:35,540
Oh, bravo.

767
00:29:35,573 --> 00:29:38,576
Wilda Taylor, wasn't
she in Sweet Mud Honey?

768
00:29:41,713 --> 00:29:43,348
Oh, and here's
a couple of guys

769
00:29:43,381 --> 00:29:45,183
from a hearty Norwegian stock.

770
00:29:45,216 --> 00:29:46,351
(laughing)

771
00:29:46,384 --> 00:29:48,386
From a story by
the writer's block.

772
00:29:49,554 --> 00:29:53,391
Oh look, the great
Monroe P. Askins.

773
00:29:53,424 --> 00:29:55,760
All the snooties
are on this page.

774
00:29:57,061 --> 00:30:00,365
Kinda sounds like a
Mazola corn oil commercial.

775
00:30:00,398 --> 00:30:02,033
Corn good, yes.

776
00:30:02,066 --> 00:30:03,368
A Japanese art director? No!

777
00:30:03,401 --> 00:30:05,036
(whispering) It's okay.

778
00:30:05,069 --> 00:30:06,371
Albert Glasser!
Bravo!

779
00:30:06,404 --> 00:30:07,272
Yay!
(clapping)

780
00:30:07,305 --> 00:30:08,540
(whistling)

781
00:30:08,573 --> 00:30:13,411
Albert Glasser,
paging Albert Glasser.

782
00:30:14,612 --> 00:30:16,714
Jack Rabin, foams
at the mouth a lot.

783
00:30:16,748 --> 00:30:17,782
(laughing)

784
00:30:17,816 --> 00:30:20,218
Aww.
(hissing)

785
00:30:20,251 --> 00:30:21,653
I like him.

786
00:30:23,955 --> 00:30:27,058
Corman the barbarian.

787
00:30:27,091 --> 00:30:28,927
Corman the employer.

788
00:30:28,960 --> 00:30:30,128
Mm-hmm
(laughing)

789
00:30:31,930 --> 00:30:34,232
In the days when
the world was young,

790
00:30:34,265 --> 00:30:35,567
Frodo...

791
00:30:35,600 --> 00:30:37,235
And the gods had not yet
abandonded the race of men...

792
00:30:37,268 --> 00:30:39,437
Gildor and Stryder
took Frodo to Rivendell.

793
00:30:39,470 --> 00:30:41,239
The great and
stalwart people.

794
00:30:41,272 --> 00:30:42,407
I'm ashamed to know that.

795
00:30:42,440 --> 00:30:43,908
Smoking rich,
robust pipe tobacco.

796
00:30:43,942 --> 00:30:45,410
Forbidding mountains could

797
00:30:45,443 --> 00:30:47,245
no longer yeild
them a livelyhood.

798
00:30:47,278 --> 00:30:49,247
They bade farewell
to their women.

799
00:30:49,280 --> 00:30:50,415
Bad?

800
00:30:50,448 --> 00:30:52,417
And ventured fearlessly
into the unknown.

801
00:30:52,450 --> 00:30:53,751
But like most
men, they got lost

802
00:30:53,785 --> 00:30:55,453
and refused to ask
for directions.

803
00:30:55,486 --> 00:30:57,255
Three years...

804
00:30:57,288 --> 00:30:59,424
A band of brave women gather,

805
00:30:59,457 --> 00:31:00,592
to decide whether they should

806
00:31:00,625 --> 00:31:02,594
embark on a search
for their men.

807
00:31:02,627 --> 00:31:04,262
- Ah, screw it.
- They cast their votes,

808
00:31:04,295 --> 00:31:06,931
in typical Viking fashion.

809
00:31:06,965 --> 00:31:08,633
I vote we go.

810
00:31:14,339 --> 00:31:15,340
We stay.

811
00:31:15,373 --> 00:31:17,108
- I'll stay, I'm in.
- No.

812
00:31:17,141 --> 00:31:18,309
Yes.

813
00:31:18,343 --> 00:31:20,845
Ooh, I hope they
start wrestling.

814
00:31:20,879 --> 00:31:23,648
Here's Martina, watch
out for that forehand.

815
00:31:24,816 --> 00:31:27,185
Oh, be careful with
that rotator cuff.

816
00:31:27,218 --> 00:31:28,620
The gods have been offended.

817
00:31:28,653 --> 00:31:30,455
It's an omen of disaster.

818
00:31:30,488 --> 00:31:31,489
It's a calamity!

819
00:31:31,522 --> 00:31:32,624
That's enough.

820
00:31:32,657 --> 00:31:35,526
I'm ashamed of the
women of Stonyold.

821
00:31:35,560 --> 00:31:37,862
This was your
proposal, Desir, not ours.

822
00:31:37,896 --> 00:31:39,297
Oh, I think I
found my favorite.

823
00:31:39,330 --> 00:31:40,865
Do you wish to die unloved?

824
00:31:40,899 --> 00:31:42,467
Connie Sellecca?

825
00:31:42,500 --> 00:31:45,303
We cannot go
against our fate.

826
00:31:45,336 --> 00:31:46,638
The gods are angry.

827
00:31:46,671 --> 00:31:49,807
The gods are angry only
at those who cower in fear.

828
00:31:49,841 --> 00:31:52,477
Vikings make their own fate.

829
00:31:52,510 --> 00:31:53,978
And their own apple sauce.

830
00:31:54,012 --> 00:31:56,714
Oh, Vikings always
fade in the second half.

831
00:31:56,748 --> 00:32:00,184
How long has it been since
our men sailed across the sea?

832
00:32:00,218 --> 00:32:01,486
- Two perms ago.
- How long have you

833
00:32:01,519 --> 00:32:02,820
waited on the cold cliffs,

834
00:32:02,854 --> 00:32:05,490
for a sight of their
returning sail?

835
00:32:05,523 --> 00:32:06,991
In vain.

836
00:32:07,025 --> 00:32:08,326
Let's put it this way,

837
00:32:08,359 --> 00:32:09,994
you're starting to
look pretty good to me.

838
00:32:10,028 --> 00:32:12,063
Is it too much to ask
the maidens of Stonyold

839
00:32:12,096 --> 00:32:13,231
to help their people?

840
00:32:13,264 --> 00:32:14,332
Is that so wrong?

841
00:32:14,365 --> 00:32:15,366
if you need an omen...

842
00:32:15,400 --> 00:32:18,236
Let's see, omen,
omen, where'd I put it?

843
00:32:18,269 --> 00:32:20,004
- Look!
- Oh, yeah.

844
00:32:20,038 --> 00:32:23,341
It is balloon!

845
00:32:23,374 --> 00:32:24,676
It's a cloud.

846
00:32:24,709 --> 00:32:26,377
Looks like a bunny.

847
00:32:26,411 --> 00:32:29,080
They need us,
as we need them.

848
00:32:29,113 --> 00:32:30,715
Like a fish needs a bicycle.

849
00:32:31,582 --> 00:32:35,086
(dramatic music)

850
00:32:35,119 --> 00:32:36,721
Now we do lawn darts.

851
00:32:36,754 --> 00:32:39,590
Thyra, you vote to go?

852
00:32:39,624 --> 00:32:41,526
Yet you have no lover over seas.

853
00:32:41,559 --> 00:32:44,429
Am I likely to find one here?

854
00:32:44,462 --> 00:32:47,365
Can I vote again?
I wanna vote again.

855
00:32:47,398 --> 00:32:49,567
Wouldn't a show
of hands be easier?

856
00:32:52,136 --> 00:32:55,073
You must decide for
yourself, little sister.

857
00:32:55,106 --> 00:32:57,742
Little sister, don't you
do what your big sister done.

858
00:33:01,245 --> 00:33:03,381
(dramatic music)

859
00:33:03,414 --> 00:33:04,782
The windup, the pitch...

860
00:33:05,483 --> 00:33:08,052
Triple 19, that's the game.

861
00:33:08,086 --> 00:33:09,620
Wow.

862
00:33:11,589 --> 00:33:14,592
To please her sister,
Asmild will destroy us all.

863
00:33:14,625 --> 00:33:17,061
Her sister's kinda spooky,
isn't she?

864
00:33:17,095 --> 00:33:18,296
Maybe not.

865
00:33:18,329 --> 00:33:20,932
See who's casting
the deciding spear.

866
00:33:22,166 --> 00:33:23,601
It's Kate Bush.

867
00:33:24,268 --> 00:33:25,570
Hounds of love.

868
00:33:25,603 --> 00:33:27,739
How do you think our
dark priestess will vote?

869
00:33:27,772 --> 00:33:29,407
How do you
handle a hungry man?

870
00:33:29,440 --> 00:33:32,276
She hates Desir
and wants her man.

871
00:33:33,778 --> 00:33:36,848
Oh, a southpaw.
Never trust a lefty.

872
00:33:38,349 --> 00:33:40,318
It's a lady of the javelin.

873
00:33:40,351 --> 00:33:41,686
(dramatic music)

874
00:33:42,453 --> 00:33:43,955
You vote to go?

875
00:33:43,988 --> 00:33:46,958
(murmuring)

876
00:33:46,991 --> 00:33:49,761
Well, she's not
coming in my car.

877
00:33:49,794 --> 00:33:52,096
Did you try to do that?

878
00:33:52,130 --> 00:33:53,431
Thank you, Enger.

879
00:33:53,464 --> 00:33:55,099
For making us laugh again.

880
00:33:55,133 --> 00:33:56,434
I want no thanks.

881
00:33:56,467 --> 00:33:58,436
The decision is made.

882
00:33:58,469 --> 00:34:00,271
Come, there's work to be done.

883
00:34:00,304 --> 00:34:03,808
Geepers, I guess we know
who turns the screws, yipes.

884
00:34:05,476 --> 00:34:06,778
(snickering)

885
00:34:06,811 --> 00:34:07,945
Hey, Todd, nice
button collection.

886
00:34:07,979 --> 00:34:09,480
Aren't you glad
she voted to go?

887
00:34:09,514 --> 00:34:11,449
Yes, but why did she do it?

888
00:34:11,482 --> 00:34:14,485
Oh, Ottar, you're
always so suspicious.

889
00:34:14,519 --> 00:34:15,653
He must be 4-F.

890
00:34:15,686 --> 00:34:16,854
I'm glad she's with us.

891
00:34:17,655 --> 00:34:18,656
Looks like a waffle.

892
00:34:18,689 --> 00:34:20,291
Desir, I'm going with you.

893
00:34:20,324 --> 00:34:21,459
No, Ottar.

894
00:34:21,492 --> 00:34:23,494
There's no need for
you to take the risk.

895
00:34:23,528 --> 00:34:25,363
Anyway, you'd never
pass the physical.

896
00:34:25,396 --> 00:34:26,964
I spoke brave words.

897
00:34:26,998 --> 00:34:29,967
But I know we face great danger.

898
00:34:30,001 --> 00:34:31,335
Great Danes?

899
00:34:33,337 --> 00:34:38,176
(humming)

900
00:34:38,209 --> 00:34:39,210
Well, it's a strange day

901
00:34:39,243 --> 00:34:41,979
when Enger the dark
gave the cause of Desir.

902
00:34:42,013 --> 00:34:43,381
What's that supposed to mean?

903
00:34:43,414 --> 00:34:44,515
She had a good idea.

904
00:34:44,549 --> 00:34:45,483
On paper, anyway.

905
00:34:45,516 --> 00:34:46,717
Oh, come my dear.

906
00:34:46,751 --> 00:34:49,687
Don't tell me you'd do
anything to help Desir.

907
00:34:50,855 --> 00:34:52,490
I think I understand.

908
00:34:52,523 --> 00:34:54,325
If at the end of the voyage,

909
00:34:54,358 --> 00:34:55,726
Vedric holds out his arms,

910
00:34:55,760 --> 00:34:58,696
and his flaxen haired beauty
is not there to unfold in them,

911
00:34:58,729 --> 00:35:02,166
perhaps the chief will look
elesewhere for comfort.

912
00:35:02,200 --> 00:35:04,535
Was that an alegory
or an extended metaphor?

913
00:35:04,569 --> 00:35:06,003
Those may be your
thoughts, Dagda,

914
00:35:06,037 --> 00:35:08,739
- but I say nothing.
- She's wearing a lady Timex.

915
00:35:10,575 --> 00:35:13,377
So the Vikings gave
us the under-wire bra.

916
00:35:13,411 --> 00:35:16,848
Gonna see Ms. Liza.
(groaning)

917
00:35:16,881 --> 00:35:18,516
Hey, I just had this dream.

918
00:35:18,549 --> 00:35:20,084
Hmm?

919
00:35:21,419 --> 00:35:25,423
You know, these new prefab
Viking ships are great.

920
00:35:26,757 --> 00:35:28,693
After having
won the spirit jug,

921
00:35:28,726 --> 00:35:30,194
the bulletin board competition
and the skip night,

922
00:35:30,228 --> 00:35:33,064
the plucky girls now will dip
into the float competition.

923
00:35:34,565 --> 00:35:36,400
Just like Iwo Jima.

924
00:35:36,434 --> 00:35:38,136
Oh, wow, man.

925
00:35:41,072 --> 00:35:43,541
And they named the
ship the Andrea what?

926
00:35:43,574 --> 00:35:44,909
Doria, Doria.

927
00:35:47,111 --> 00:35:49,280
Break out the hot
dish, I got bars.

928
00:35:51,582 --> 00:35:53,751
What about the christening?

929
00:35:55,086 --> 00:35:56,754
If this were
reality, they'd still be

930
00:35:56,787 --> 00:35:59,423
waiting for each
other to get ready.

931
00:35:59,457 --> 00:36:00,558
(yelling)

932
00:36:00,591 --> 00:36:01,459
Uh-oh.

933
00:36:02,927 --> 00:36:05,463
This is going well, so far.

934
00:36:05,496 --> 00:36:07,431
(laughing)
Run! Run!

935
00:36:07,465 --> 00:36:09,233
Quick, get that
oar in the water!

936
00:36:09,267 --> 00:36:11,402
You don't have
an oar in the water,

937
00:36:11,435 --> 00:36:12,403
if you know what I mean.

938
00:36:12,436 --> 00:36:14,105
I'm getting it now!

939
00:36:15,439 --> 00:36:16,908
(gurgling)

940
00:36:16,941 --> 00:36:18,576
Come on. Come on.

941
00:36:18,609 --> 00:36:20,478
Come here. Come here.

942
00:36:20,511 --> 00:36:22,013
Come on, get in dummy.

943
00:36:23,114 --> 00:36:24,582
You alright?

944
00:36:24,615 --> 00:36:26,250
Desir, this is hopeless.

945
00:36:26,284 --> 00:36:28,452
We can't handle this
boat with just an oar.

946
00:36:30,121 --> 00:36:30,955
We've go to.

947
00:36:32,123 --> 00:36:34,792
Well, no sense swimming
20 feet back to get it.

948
00:36:36,460 --> 00:36:39,797
Hey, I've got a wild idea.
Let's put on a show.

949
00:36:41,165 --> 00:36:44,435
Oh, they got that
dragon in Tijuana.

950
00:36:44,468 --> 00:36:46,804
(dramatic music)

951
00:36:51,709 --> 00:36:53,511
Albert Glasser at his best.

952
00:36:56,480 --> 00:36:59,183
(yawning)
(coughing)

953
00:37:00,318 --> 00:37:01,786
Say, look at the
prow on that steamer.

954
00:37:01,819 --> 00:37:03,154
Hey, let's climb aboard.

955
00:37:04,488 --> 00:37:05,623
It's not the
size of the boat.

956
00:37:05,656 --> 00:37:07,291
It's the motion of the ocean.

957
00:37:07,325 --> 00:37:08,292
- Oh.
- Mm-hmm.

958
00:37:08,326 --> 00:37:10,828
Now you'll be
warm, little sister.

959
00:37:10,861 --> 00:37:12,463
What would I do without you?

960
00:37:12,496 --> 00:37:14,165
Well, you could wear a coat.

961
00:37:14,198 --> 00:37:15,499
Go to sleep.

962
00:37:16,667 --> 00:37:19,804
Do you really think
we'll ever find the men?

963
00:37:19,837 --> 00:37:21,305
Of course we will, Asmild.

964
00:37:21,339 --> 00:37:23,341
That's what these
cruises are for, dear.

965
00:37:24,842 --> 00:37:27,478
Then Vedric will
ask you to marry him.

966
00:37:27,511 --> 00:37:29,146
Yes, he will.

967
00:37:29,180 --> 00:37:32,316
What is it like to love
a man as you love Vedric?

968
00:37:32,350 --> 00:37:34,485
Know what I mean?
Nudge Nudge. Wink. Wink.

969
00:37:34,518 --> 00:37:35,820
What it really means.

970
00:37:35,853 --> 00:37:37,855
Some day you'll know.

971
00:37:41,192 --> 00:37:43,527
He'll give me a
ring of betrothal.

972
00:37:44,695 --> 00:37:47,498
And claim me forever as his own.

973
00:37:47,531 --> 00:37:48,666
As a tax deduction.

974
00:37:48,699 --> 00:37:53,537
(Mystery Science Theater
3000 theme music)

975
00:37:57,908 --> 00:38:00,678
I'm a bear. I'm a
bog old bear. (growling)

976
00:38:00,711 --> 00:38:03,014
Yeah, this is
getting weird, you guys.

977
00:38:03,047 --> 00:38:05,449
There's a silouette of theater
seats in front of that movie.

978
00:38:09,086 --> 00:38:12,056
Ottar, what are
you doing here?

979
00:38:12,089 --> 00:38:13,524
And you're wearing my things!

980
00:38:13,557 --> 00:38:14,592
What are you doing here?

981
00:38:14,625 --> 00:38:16,594
Starving. Have you
got any extra food?

982
00:38:16,627 --> 00:38:17,762
Yes.

983
00:38:17,795 --> 00:38:19,230
Don't tell the
others I'm here, please.

984
00:38:19,263 --> 00:38:21,032
We're still too close to shore.

985
00:38:21,065 --> 00:38:22,700
They might decide
to take me back.

986
00:38:22,733 --> 00:38:25,569
Todd! Hey, he's
looking up her dress.

987
00:38:28,406 --> 00:38:29,540
Hi, how you doing?

988
00:38:29,573 --> 00:38:30,875
You, know I hate men, don't you?

989
00:38:30,908 --> 00:38:32,209
Isn't Todd just a jerk?

990
00:38:32,243 --> 00:38:33,711
Something
bothering you Thyra?

991
00:38:33,744 --> 00:38:35,880
Oh, I was just
fixing these ropes.

992
00:38:35,913 --> 00:38:39,083
Oh, I thought I heard
you talking to yourself.

993
00:38:39,116 --> 00:38:40,751
I guess I do that sometimes.

994
00:38:40,785 --> 00:38:42,553
Well, see that it stops.

995
00:38:42,586 --> 00:38:43,888
I just wanted another fur.

996
00:38:43,921 --> 00:38:45,056
Well, why don't
you take this one?

997
00:38:45,089 --> 00:38:46,791
These are still
wet from the spray.

998
00:38:46,824 --> 00:38:48,059
I told you to
hang off further.

999
00:38:48,092 --> 00:38:49,427
How long have you been a Viking?

1000
00:38:51,495 --> 00:38:52,997
(giggling)

1001
00:38:56,600 --> 00:38:57,768
All clear.

1002
00:38:57,802 --> 00:38:59,470
Hey, got any rye
crisps? I'm starved.

1003
00:38:59,503 --> 00:39:01,405
She gives me the creeps.

1004
00:39:01,439 --> 00:39:02,473
You and me both.

1005
00:39:03,341 --> 00:39:04,575
Where's the food?

1006
00:39:04,608 --> 00:39:06,610
Oh, can I get you a beer too?

1007
00:39:06,644 --> 00:39:07,611
Is that all you men think about?

1008
00:39:07,645 --> 00:39:08,913
Help yourself.

1009
00:39:08,946 --> 00:39:12,583
It's Todd Knotts, as
the reluctant Viking.

1010
00:39:12,616 --> 00:39:14,752
Ottar, why did you stow away?

1011
00:39:14,785 --> 00:39:16,921
Somebody has to
protect you women.

1012
00:39:16,954 --> 00:39:17,988
(kissing sounds)

1013
00:39:18,022 --> 00:39:18,923
It's ridiculous
not to have a man

1014
00:39:18,956 --> 00:39:20,091
along on a dangerous voyage.

1015
00:39:20,124 --> 00:39:21,592
Or at least a patron
of the arts, anyhow.

1016
00:39:21,625 --> 00:39:22,526
Ridiculous not to have a man.

1017
00:39:24,028 --> 00:39:26,430
- Thyra?
- Hmm?

1018
00:39:26,464 --> 00:39:27,832
This kipper stinks.

1019
00:39:27,865 --> 00:39:29,266
When I saw you, I
thought it was over for me.

1020
00:39:29,300 --> 00:39:31,969
Lucky I recognized
you in time.

1021
00:39:32,002 --> 00:39:33,437
I'd have my dagger
in your throat.

1022
00:39:33,471 --> 00:39:34,438
Ooh!

1023
00:39:34,472 --> 00:39:35,639
I didn't mean that.

1024
00:39:36,674 --> 00:39:38,943
It's just that I
didn't think you'd

1025
00:39:38,976 --> 00:39:41,045
protect me from Enger
the way that you did.

1026
00:39:42,146 --> 00:39:43,481
You've always seemed
so forbidding,

1027
00:39:43,514 --> 00:39:45,649
that I've always been
afraid to talk to you.

1028
00:39:45,683 --> 00:39:46,817
Kinda like Mom.

1029
00:39:46,851 --> 00:39:48,319
There's nothing
to be afraid of.

1030
00:39:51,489 --> 00:39:52,623
Come on.

1031
00:39:52,656 --> 00:39:53,691
- Get in.
- Get some sleep.

1032
00:39:55,159 --> 00:39:56,293
- Good night.
- Tuck me in,

1033
00:39:56,327 --> 00:39:57,561
and call me a bad boy, please?

1034
00:40:00,998 --> 00:40:02,366
Oh, I can't breathe.

1035
00:40:02,400 --> 00:40:06,203
How do bears live in
these things? Gee!

1036
00:40:06,237 --> 00:40:09,507
(singing) Go to
sleep, my sweet Todd.

1037
00:40:09,540 --> 00:40:10,474
Hey, wait a minute.

1038
00:40:10,508 --> 00:40:14,145
(peaceful music)

1039
00:40:14,178 --> 00:40:15,880
The women of Green Peace.

1040
00:40:15,913 --> 00:40:17,515
Look, it's a monster!

1041
00:40:18,015 --> 00:40:19,984
It's a sea serpent!

1042
00:40:20,017 --> 00:40:23,487
No, we're whales!
Save us! Save us!

1043
00:40:23,521 --> 00:40:25,055
(laughing)

1044
00:40:25,089 --> 00:40:26,657
Women, you'd think they
never saw a whale before.

1045
00:40:26,690 --> 00:40:27,658
Who let you out of your skin?

1046
00:40:27,691 --> 00:40:28,826
They haven't, Ottar.

1047
00:40:28,859 --> 00:40:30,828
Not everyone is as
well-traveled as you.

1048
00:40:30,861 --> 00:40:32,530
Or as... Oh, never mind.

1049
00:40:32,563 --> 00:40:33,497
Get these swords polished.

1050
00:40:33,531 --> 00:40:36,167
Stow aways must work
for their passage.

1051
00:40:36,200 --> 00:40:38,569
Nevertheless, some day
you'll be glad I came along.

1052
00:40:38,602 --> 00:40:41,405
Why must they laugh
at my mighty sword?

1053
00:40:47,912 --> 00:40:50,047
Two years before them mast.

1054
00:40:51,882 --> 00:40:53,551
(dramatic music)
I'm voguing.

1055
00:40:57,755 --> 00:40:59,190
Hmm, it's a big ship.

1056
00:40:59,223 --> 00:41:01,125
Nobody will see me doing this.

1057
00:41:05,396 --> 00:41:08,032
We think the joke
will be on Sanda.

1058
00:41:08,065 --> 00:41:09,400
Oh! (laughing)

1059
00:41:10,768 --> 00:41:12,036
Are you hurt?

1060
00:41:12,069 --> 00:41:14,738
The yard. The
line must've slipped.

1061
00:41:16,273 --> 00:41:17,708
This couldn't have slipped.

1062
00:41:17,741 --> 00:41:18,876
I made it fast, myself.

1063
00:41:18,909 --> 00:41:20,044
That's why it did slip.

1064
00:41:20,077 --> 00:41:21,412
You alright?

1065
00:41:21,445 --> 00:41:23,581
Never mind, let's just
get the sail back up.

1066
00:41:32,089 --> 00:41:33,958
Look there's a
sale at Penny's.

1067
00:41:33,991 --> 00:41:35,092
- Get it?
- Yeah.

1068
00:41:35,125 --> 00:41:37,595
I am hot. I turn myself on.

1069
00:41:43,167 --> 00:41:46,403
This looks like
a job for Superman.

1070
00:41:46,437 --> 00:41:49,240
Wait, she takes the belt off,
but leaves those heavy chains.

1071
00:41:49,273 --> 00:41:50,741
That'll sink her to
the bottom, for sure.

1072
00:41:50,774 --> 00:41:51,475
Head first.

1073
00:41:52,276 --> 00:41:53,644
Person overboard!

1074
00:41:56,180 --> 00:41:59,617
Diana Nyad is attempting
to swim the channel again.

1075
00:42:01,352 --> 00:42:04,922
Oh, look, it says
S.S. Minnow on it.

1076
00:42:04,955 --> 00:42:06,790
Flipper, no!

1077
00:42:10,127 --> 00:42:14,431
(dramatic music)

1078
00:42:14,465 --> 00:42:16,934
It's okay, just do
what Harvey Mackay says!

1079
00:42:16,967 --> 00:42:18,135
Swim with the sharks!

1080
00:42:18,168 --> 00:42:19,803
That's a book
reference, isn't it?

1081
00:42:19,837 --> 00:42:21,105
Right.

1082
00:42:21,138 --> 00:42:23,841
That's not a shark,
it's a rock lobster!

1083
00:42:23,874 --> 00:42:25,509
That's a rock
reference, isn't it?

1084
00:42:25,543 --> 00:42:26,710
Mm-hmm.

1085
00:42:29,313 --> 00:42:31,115
We're gonna get a
bigger bait, right?

1086
00:42:31,148 --> 00:42:33,517
- You alright?
- That's a movie reference.

1087
00:42:33,551 --> 00:42:34,618
Yeah.

1088
00:42:34,652 --> 00:42:35,819
Look there!

1089
00:42:36,820 --> 00:42:39,623
Oh, no! It's the rinse cycle!

1090
00:42:39,657 --> 00:42:40,624
It can't be.

1091
00:42:40,658 --> 00:42:42,626
I always thought it
was just a sailor's tale.

1092
00:42:42,660 --> 00:42:43,527
But it's true.

1093
00:42:43,561 --> 00:42:44,862
(roaring)

1094
00:42:44,895 --> 00:42:46,130
Let's get out of here!

1095
00:42:46,163 --> 00:42:47,364
Man the oars!

1096
00:42:47,398 --> 00:42:49,333
Or woman the oars,
as the case may be.

1097
00:42:50,334 --> 00:42:52,503
We're drifting into it!

1098
00:42:52,536 --> 00:42:53,404
Huh?

1099
00:42:54,672 --> 00:42:56,140
Make a dam, Shawn!

1100
00:42:56,173 --> 00:42:57,541
That's what I
thought he said.

1101
00:42:59,510 --> 00:43:03,213
And that brochure said
old-world charm, pooey!

1102
00:43:03,247 --> 00:43:04,848
Her job was to look ravished.

1103
00:43:09,253 --> 00:43:12,590
(mock wind blowing)

1104
00:43:16,894 --> 00:43:18,495
Call me Ishmael.

1105
00:43:18,529 --> 00:43:19,663
Todd Ishmael.

1106
00:43:19,697 --> 00:43:20,564
(laughing)

1107
00:43:21,865 --> 00:43:24,835
This is actually pretty
extravegant for Corman.

1108
00:43:24,868 --> 00:43:26,337
Yeah, sure is.

1109
00:43:26,370 --> 00:43:27,705
I'm your boyfriend now.

1110
00:43:28,539 --> 00:43:29,907
(laughing)

1111
00:43:30,874 --> 00:43:32,076
Oh, look at their hair.

1112
00:43:32,109 --> 00:43:33,911
You can tell the
wind's really blowing.

1113
00:43:33,944 --> 00:43:35,713
- Oh, no!
- No!

1114
00:43:40,784 --> 00:43:43,554
I'm coming bitty boy!

1115
00:43:44,388 --> 00:43:47,391
It can sense
fear, so stay calm.

1116
00:43:49,226 --> 00:43:51,195
Oh, it must've
hit the gas tank.

1117
00:43:51,228 --> 00:43:53,397
Oh, no.
(screaming)

1118
00:43:53,430 --> 00:43:55,566
Ah criminy, what a crappy day.

1119
00:43:55,599 --> 00:43:57,434
Well, at least it's
not raining, eh?

1120
00:43:58,969 --> 00:44:00,371
(gurgling)

1121
00:44:00,404 --> 00:44:02,740
Light the shoe box and
throw it in the toilet.

1122
00:44:04,408 --> 00:44:07,044
At 11:15, the old
cook came on deck

1123
00:44:07,077 --> 00:44:08,946
and said women it's
been good to know you.

1124
00:44:08,979 --> 00:44:10,881
(laughing)

1125
00:44:10,914 --> 00:44:13,250
Don't flush!
(gurgling)

1126
00:44:18,422 --> 00:44:20,391
By the shores
of Gitche Gumee,

1127
00:44:20,424 --> 00:44:22,426
by the shining big sea waters...

1128
00:44:22,459 --> 00:44:25,062
Meanwhile in
From Here to Eternity...

1129
00:44:25,095 --> 00:44:28,565
(spitting)

1130
00:44:28,599 --> 00:44:29,800
I feel gritty.

1131
00:44:29,833 --> 00:44:31,935
I'll never drink vodka
from a watermelon again.

1132
00:44:31,969 --> 00:44:33,303
I don't know where we are,

1133
00:44:33,337 --> 00:44:35,572
but I've got a feeling we're
near the Kennedy compound.

1134
00:44:35,606 --> 00:44:38,609
Oh great, it's
a private beach.

1135
00:44:40,110 --> 00:44:40,944
Faced again.

1136
00:44:42,312 --> 00:44:43,414
Tough surfers.

1137
00:44:43,447 --> 00:44:45,516
Mind if we dance
with your dates?

1138
00:44:48,352 --> 00:44:50,788
Thanks Todd, good effort.

1139
00:44:50,821 --> 00:44:52,089
(laughing)
Pathetic.

1140
00:44:52,122 --> 00:44:54,291
Oh, I think he
means to follow him.

1141
00:44:54,324 --> 00:44:55,626
I guess so.

1142
00:44:58,328 --> 00:44:59,963
(whip cracking)

1143
00:44:59,997 --> 00:45:01,298
Who is he whipping?

1144
00:45:01,331 --> 00:45:04,435
That Foley guy is just like
way off script, isn't he?

1145
00:45:04,468 --> 00:45:07,304
Oh, good. They've washed
up in Monument Valley.

1146
00:45:10,474 --> 00:45:12,609
Todd, now get back
in line. Quit clowning.

1147
00:45:12,643 --> 00:45:14,545
(laughing)
You little scamp.

1148
00:45:15,479 --> 00:45:17,181
This story is
right out of Homer.

1149
00:45:17,214 --> 00:45:18,482
And Jethro?

1150
00:45:22,052 --> 00:45:24,955
I hate these
dude ranchers, you?

1151
00:45:24,988 --> 00:45:26,156
Are you afraid?

1152
00:45:26,190 --> 00:45:28,358
I'm too numb to be afraid.

1153
00:45:28,392 --> 00:45:31,628
Get captured often?
Live around here?

1154
00:45:31,662 --> 00:45:32,963
Of me?

1155
00:45:32,996 --> 00:45:35,466
Yes, all they can do is
kill me or make a slave of me.

1156
00:45:35,499 --> 00:45:36,834
Either one's fine by me.

1157
00:45:37,901 --> 00:45:39,903
I'd like to see them try.

1158
00:45:43,540 --> 00:45:46,510
Kirk Douglas and the Viking.

1159
00:45:48,879 --> 00:45:49,980
Get up!

1160
00:45:50,013 --> 00:45:52,015
Which one of us?

1161
00:45:55,385 --> 00:45:57,721
Like I'm almost
sure, this place bites.

1162
00:45:59,890 --> 00:46:04,361
These are the guys that
gave us Mongolian stir-fry.

1163
00:46:05,763 --> 00:46:06,864
Scum.

1164
00:46:08,031 --> 00:46:09,767
They can be handled.

1165
00:46:10,534 --> 00:46:11,702
They're only men.

1166
00:46:11,735 --> 00:46:14,371
Oh, Gloria Steinem.
(laughing)

1167
00:46:18,275 --> 00:46:20,711
Looks like a pump,
feels like a sneaker.

1168
00:46:28,418 --> 00:46:30,888
Yonder lies the
castle of my father.

1169
00:46:32,389 --> 00:46:34,358
Okay, well, yonder
lies the matte painting

1170
00:46:34,391 --> 00:46:36,193
of the castle of my father.

1171
00:46:36,226 --> 00:46:37,361
(whip cracking)
Ow!

1172
00:46:37,394 --> 00:46:38,362
Those were just for fun.

1173
00:46:38,395 --> 00:46:39,530
Those were for me.

1174
00:46:39,563 --> 00:46:40,898
That was way off
script, on those.

1175
00:46:50,741 --> 00:46:53,577
(humming)

1176
00:46:54,244 --> 00:46:55,412
I love these walking scenes.

1177
00:46:55,445 --> 00:46:57,381
And speaking of walking, gang...

1178
00:46:57,414 --> 00:46:58,582
Yeah.

1179
00:46:58,615 --> 00:47:00,217
Forget that guy.
He's not in that movie.

1180
00:47:00,250 --> 00:47:01,618
(whip cracking)
Ooh!

1181
00:47:01,652 --> 00:47:04,087
They can do such good things
with walking scenes though.

1182
00:47:06,423 --> 00:47:11,628
(Mystery Science Theater
3000 theme music)

1183
00:47:21,271 --> 00:47:22,472
Waffles.

1184
00:47:22,506 --> 00:47:25,642
(alarm buzzing)
(yelling)

1185
00:47:27,444 --> 00:47:32,616
(doors grinding)

1186
00:47:33,450 --> 00:47:35,152
- Oh, hi.
- You okay?

1187
00:47:35,185 --> 00:47:36,987
I just did a
really nutty thing,

1188
00:47:37,020 --> 00:47:38,789
with Cambot, about waffles.

1189
00:47:40,791 --> 00:47:42,826
Are they meeting
their mothers already?

1190
00:47:44,628 --> 00:47:46,463
He's got a sweaty back.

1191
00:47:46,496 --> 00:47:48,298
Well, he's been sitting
in the saddle all day.

1192
00:47:48,332 --> 00:47:50,267
(dramatic music)

1193
00:47:50,300 --> 00:47:51,635
Hey!

1194
00:47:54,304 --> 00:47:55,472
You're gonna stay
at the Red Roof Inn,

1195
00:47:55,505 --> 00:47:56,807
whether you like it or not!

1196
00:47:58,709 --> 00:48:00,277
Oh, no, they're being forced

1197
00:48:00,310 --> 00:48:02,279
to attend the
renaissance festival.

1198
00:48:02,312 --> 00:48:03,780
Would you look at
the prices in here,

1199
00:48:03,814 --> 00:48:05,549
just for a
continental breakfast.

1200
00:48:07,317 --> 00:48:08,685
This is a very
impressive buidling,

1201
00:48:08,719 --> 00:48:10,354
for these heathens
to have built.

1202
00:48:10,387 --> 00:48:11,455
Maybe they didn't.

1203
00:48:11,488 --> 00:48:13,457
- What do you mean?
- They're renting.

1204
00:48:13,490 --> 00:48:15,459
Vikings built this,
not those savages.

1205
00:48:15,492 --> 00:48:17,461
Vikings have never
been in this land.

1206
00:48:17,494 --> 00:48:19,296
We'd have heard it
sung in the sagas.

1207
00:48:19,329 --> 00:48:21,131
Only if they came back.

1208
00:48:21,164 --> 00:48:22,466
To Brylcreem.

1209
00:48:22,499 --> 00:48:26,336
(dramatic music)

1210
00:48:26,370 --> 00:48:27,871
Hello, I'm the concierge.

1211
00:48:31,341 --> 00:48:32,843
Oh, sorry about the costume.

1212
00:48:32,876 --> 00:48:35,379
Corman's poodle died and he
doesn't like to waste anything.

1213
00:48:35,412 --> 00:48:39,583
I am Inspector Clouseau,
I want to see Todd.

1214
00:48:41,685 --> 00:48:42,686
I could never
quite get over that

1215
00:48:42,719 --> 00:48:44,388
breakup of Sonny and Cher.

1216
00:48:44,421 --> 00:48:45,856
Hello, girls!

1217
00:48:48,058 --> 00:48:49,026
No.

1218
00:48:49,059 --> 00:48:50,027
Yes.

1219
00:48:51,194 --> 00:48:52,696
(growling)

1220
00:48:53,597 --> 00:48:55,699
You! What's the
fifth general order?

1221
00:48:59,603 --> 00:49:01,571
Mmm, Oil of Olay.

1222
00:49:05,208 --> 00:49:07,010
Oh, dear, dear, split ends.

1223
00:49:07,044 --> 00:49:09,546
Clip those and try
a hot oil treatment, dear.

1224
00:49:14,084 --> 00:49:15,752
You, well, never mind.

1225
00:49:19,423 --> 00:49:20,590
- Whoa.
- Keep your filthy hands

1226
00:49:20,624 --> 00:49:22,526
off of her, you
big slobbering dog.

1227
00:49:22,559 --> 00:49:23,694
Ottar, be careful
of these animals.

1228
00:49:23,727 --> 00:49:25,729
If you only understood
a civilized tongue,

1229
00:49:25,762 --> 00:49:28,098
I'd tell you some
things, you big slob.

1230
00:49:29,566 --> 00:49:31,068
And what would you tell me?

1231
00:49:31,101 --> 00:49:34,538
I would say that you're
actually a nice slob. (laughing)

1232
00:49:34,571 --> 00:49:36,039
You speak our language.

1233
00:49:36,073 --> 00:49:38,375
A few words I learned
from some of your countrymen.

1234
00:49:38,408 --> 00:49:40,277
Were they men of Stonyold?

1235
00:49:42,412 --> 00:49:45,215
Did their craft bear
the dragon shield?

1236
00:49:45,248 --> 00:49:46,249
I really don't know.

1237
00:49:46,283 --> 00:49:47,551
And I don't really care.

1238
00:49:47,584 --> 00:49:50,387
This happens to all
craft that enter our waters.

1239
00:49:50,420 --> 00:49:51,788
They're swalloed by the vortex.

1240
00:49:51,822 --> 00:49:54,424
Destroyed by the great beast.

1241
00:49:54,458 --> 00:49:55,459
Oh, god, your
hair smells good.

1242
00:49:55,492 --> 00:49:58,395
- Some were saved?
- A handful.

1243
00:49:58,428 --> 00:50:00,063
Who? Who was saved?

1244
00:50:00,097 --> 00:50:01,098
What were their names?

1245
00:50:01,131 --> 00:50:02,399
Well, let's see, a guy
named Jeff, and Murry,

1246
00:50:02,432 --> 00:50:04,101
oh, and a really fun
guy named Chopper.

1247
00:50:04,134 --> 00:50:06,103
Ah, Senya, my son.

1248
00:50:07,471 --> 00:50:09,306
Freddie Mercury?

1249
00:50:09,339 --> 00:50:11,241
Father, the
horns have sounded

1250
00:50:11,274 --> 00:50:12,576
the boar hunt, twice already.

1251
00:50:12,609 --> 00:50:13,977
When are you coming?

1252
00:50:14,011 --> 00:50:15,612
Soon. Soon, Senya.

1253
00:50:15,645 --> 00:50:16,580
Soon, Senya.

1254
00:50:16,613 --> 00:50:17,581
Gee, you're a queer old duck.

1255
00:50:17,614 --> 00:50:19,816
As you see, we have visitors.

1256
00:50:21,284 --> 00:50:23,587
Are these the new slaves?

1257
00:50:23,620 --> 00:50:25,622
Guests, guests, my boy.

1258
00:50:26,490 --> 00:50:29,159
They'll ride with
us on the boar hunt.

1259
00:50:29,192 --> 00:50:30,660
But they're women!

1260
00:50:30,694 --> 00:50:32,963
Don't hate me
because I'm beautiful.

1261
00:50:37,334 --> 00:50:38,502
Women...

1262
00:50:38,535 --> 00:50:39,770
I know someone who could use

1263
00:50:39,803 --> 00:50:41,805
a little hostility counseling.

1264
00:50:42,639 --> 00:50:44,775
Viking women, my boy.

1265
00:50:44,808 --> 00:50:46,309
They're not like other women.

1266
00:50:46,343 --> 00:50:47,511
They make great waffles.

1267
00:50:47,544 --> 00:50:48,445
- We'll have great sport.
- For breakfast.

1268
00:50:48,478 --> 00:50:49,446
Mm-hmm.

1269
00:50:49,479 --> 00:50:50,981
(mock growling)

1270
00:50:52,482 --> 00:50:54,451
Ottar, look at his arm.

1271
00:50:54,484 --> 00:50:56,286
That's Vedric's bracelet.

1272
00:50:56,319 --> 00:50:58,789
Visitors don't come
to our shores very often.

1273
00:50:58,822 --> 00:50:59,956
In the off-season.

1274
00:50:59,990 --> 00:51:00,624
We'd be happy to
have your company,

1275
00:51:00,657 --> 00:51:01,958
on our boar hunt.

1276
00:51:01,992 --> 00:51:04,795
But perhaps you're too tired,
from your long journey.

1277
00:51:04,828 --> 00:51:06,463
Vikings are never tired.

1278
00:51:06,496 --> 00:51:07,497
We rule!

1279
00:51:07,531 --> 00:51:08,632
When do we start?

1280
00:51:08,665 --> 00:51:10,534
- Immediately.
- If not sooner.

1281
00:51:10,567 --> 00:51:11,635
We accept with pleasure.

1282
00:51:11,668 --> 00:51:13,670
Do I bring you
pleasure? (laughing)

1283
00:51:13,703 --> 00:51:15,372
I'll order the horses.

1284
00:51:15,405 --> 00:51:17,808
- Of courses.
- Zarko...

1285
00:51:17,841 --> 00:51:19,976
Nice going, Todd
over there. I like that.

1286
00:51:20,010 --> 00:51:21,344
Ottar, why in the name of...

1287
00:51:22,412 --> 00:51:23,547
It'll be much
easier to find out

1288
00:51:23,580 --> 00:51:25,315
what's going on
inside that cave,

1289
00:51:25,348 --> 00:51:26,483
with them busy at the hunt.

1290
00:51:26,516 --> 00:51:31,354
(horn music)
(mocking music)

1291
00:51:33,557 --> 00:51:34,991
Unleash the dogs!

1292
00:51:35,025 --> 00:51:36,093
Both of them!

1293
00:51:39,362 --> 00:51:41,231
Bring the pooper-scooper.

1294
00:51:47,037 --> 00:51:48,672
And coming around
the clubhouse turn,

1295
00:51:48,705 --> 00:51:50,540
it's Apartment-house
with plenty of room.

1296
00:51:50,574 --> 00:51:51,541
And Girdle in the stretch.

1297
00:51:51,575 --> 00:51:52,843
Bird poop on the track.

1298
00:51:52,876 --> 00:51:55,679
And coming up the
rear is Beetle-bomb.

1299
00:51:55,712 --> 00:51:58,715
Hey, it's the dogs
from Teenage Caveman.

1300
00:52:01,618 --> 00:52:04,254
(mocking horns blowing)

1301
00:52:06,056 --> 00:52:08,024
It's bronco
Todd, to the rescue!

1302
00:52:08,058 --> 00:52:08,925
Yee-hoo!

1303
00:52:10,060 --> 00:52:10,427
Hyo!

1304
00:52:11,628 --> 00:52:14,064
Oh, how appropriate,
a boar in a Corman film.

1305
00:52:14,097 --> 00:52:15,232
(giggling)

1306
00:52:15,265 --> 00:52:16,967
(barking)

1307
00:52:20,637 --> 00:52:23,106
It's Arnold. Run Arnold!

1308
00:52:23,907 --> 00:52:26,910
(mock horse sounds)

1309
00:52:28,078 --> 00:52:31,414
Looks like Mr. Ziffle got
drunk, and painted Arnold.

1310
00:52:34,417 --> 00:52:36,419
(snorting)

1311
00:52:37,988 --> 00:52:38,955
(yelling)

1312
00:52:38,989 --> 00:52:39,723
Ow!

1313
00:52:39,756 --> 00:52:41,258
(laughing)

1314
00:52:43,760 --> 00:52:45,762
(mocking horns blowing)

1315
00:52:46,763 --> 00:52:48,398
Oh, my back.

1316
00:52:48,431 --> 00:52:49,900
Oh, Daddy, I
don't wanna hunt.

1317
00:52:49,933 --> 00:52:52,569
I wanna be a poet, a
singer, a dancer, an actor.

1318
00:52:52,602 --> 00:52:54,738
(mock crying) Ow,
it's on my butt.

1319
00:52:54,771 --> 00:52:56,239
(snorting) You want fame?

1320
00:52:56,273 --> 00:52:58,108
Well, fame costs. And here's
where you start paying.

1321
00:53:03,780 --> 00:53:05,916
I thought that little
piggy went to market.

1322
00:53:05,949 --> 00:53:07,450
He stayed home, obviously.

1323
00:53:08,518 --> 00:53:10,120
Oh, I tore my shirts.

1324
00:53:13,456 --> 00:53:14,591
Help me, please!

1325
00:53:14,624 --> 00:53:16,860
I'm being attacked
by a ham sandwich!

1326
00:53:18,461 --> 00:53:20,430
(squealing)

1327
00:53:20,463 --> 00:53:23,433
Come on, squeal
like Ned Beatty.

1328
00:53:23,466 --> 00:53:24,634
Oh, look at this.

1329
00:53:24,668 --> 00:53:26,102
Corman got his shot,
catered the crew,

1330
00:53:26,136 --> 00:53:28,471
and got costume for Teenage
Caveman, all in one bit.

1331
00:53:30,173 --> 00:53:31,274
- A girl!
- Ooh!

1332
00:53:31,308 --> 00:53:34,778
A girl had to save
a Grimolt warrior?

1333
00:53:34,811 --> 00:53:37,013
My father will never forgive me.

1334
00:53:37,047 --> 00:53:39,115
Would you rather have
been killed by the boar?

1335
00:53:39,149 --> 00:53:40,450
Besides, what's
so terrible about

1336
00:53:40,483 --> 00:53:41,985
a girl being a good hunter?

1337
00:53:42,018 --> 00:53:44,120
But you don't understand.

1338
00:53:44,154 --> 00:53:45,455
I'm a prince!

1339
00:53:45,488 --> 00:53:46,957
A live prince.

1340
00:53:46,990 --> 00:53:50,460
No, according to Grimolt law,

1341
00:53:50,493 --> 00:53:51,628
I'll be banished
from my country,

1342
00:53:51,661 --> 00:53:53,296
in complete disgrace!

1343
00:53:53,330 --> 00:53:55,799
Why didn't you mind
your own business!

1344
00:53:55,832 --> 00:53:57,133
It's Carol Channing.

1345
00:53:57,167 --> 00:53:59,502
Oh that's a lovely
pantsuit you're wearing.

1346
00:54:00,670 --> 00:54:02,038
Alright.

1347
00:54:03,506 --> 00:54:05,976
Here, you killed the
boar with that spear.

1348
00:54:06,009 --> 00:54:08,311
Oh, wait, what about the
spear that's in the boar?

1349
00:54:08,345 --> 00:54:09,312
Continuity.

1350
00:54:09,346 --> 00:54:11,648
Senya, where did you
get this bracelet?

1351
00:54:11,681 --> 00:54:12,682
From a
yellow-haired captive--

1352
00:54:12,716 --> 00:54:14,517
- Ramfest.
- Why?

1353
00:54:14,551 --> 00:54:16,353
Where is he? Is he alive?

1354
00:54:16,386 --> 00:54:17,654
Are there more than one?

1355
00:54:17,687 --> 00:54:19,489
Someone's coming.
Leave me alone.

1356
00:54:19,522 --> 00:54:20,657
I'll harm you.

1357
00:54:20,690 --> 00:54:23,059
Is this how a prince
repays his gratitude?

1358
00:54:24,194 --> 00:54:26,162
Alright, come
to my room later,

1359
00:54:26,196 --> 00:54:27,497
and I'll tell you
as much as I can.

1360
00:54:27,530 --> 00:54:30,066
But don't get caught, or
we'll both be in trouble.

1361
00:54:30,100 --> 00:54:33,703
My father forbids me
to talk to people.

1362
00:54:33,737 --> 00:54:35,538
Thanks, Pam,
you're a real pip.

1363
00:54:38,375 --> 00:54:41,511
Give it up for my posse!
Whoop! Whoop! Whoop!

1364
00:54:41,544 --> 00:54:43,413
Senya, are you alright?

1365
00:54:43,446 --> 00:54:45,749
- I'm fine, Father.
- I'm fabulous.

1366
00:54:45,782 --> 00:54:47,083
What did you expect?

1367
00:54:48,885 --> 00:54:50,453
(screaming)

1368
00:54:52,455 --> 00:54:53,590
Oh, sorry.

1369
00:54:53,623 --> 00:54:55,692
Senya, you killed a boar!

1370
00:54:55,725 --> 00:54:57,527
Oui, I did do that thing.

1371
00:54:57,560 --> 00:54:59,262
My son is a mighty hunter!

1372
00:55:00,230 --> 00:55:01,531
To the victor!

1373
00:55:01,564 --> 00:55:02,866
Three cheers for
captain Spalding.

1374
00:55:02,899 --> 00:55:05,902
(cheering)

1375
00:55:06,569 --> 00:55:08,204
(lively music)

1376
00:55:08,238 --> 00:55:10,073
Oh, it's a Theta Chi party.

1377
00:55:13,643 --> 00:55:14,577
Groovy tunes.

1378
00:55:15,745 --> 00:55:17,380
Hey, look, where's Tony Martin?

1379
00:55:17,414 --> 00:55:18,982
He's gotta be around
here somewhere.

1380
00:55:20,417 --> 00:55:21,584
Hmm.

1381
00:55:23,086 --> 00:55:25,255
Isadora Duncan, tonight
at the Viking lodge.

1382
00:55:25,288 --> 00:55:26,723
Sounds like the
Rocky Show music.

1383
00:55:26,756 --> 00:55:30,093
(humming)

1384
00:55:36,433 --> 00:55:37,934
Senator, no!

1385
00:55:40,770 --> 00:55:42,305
You know, I
really envy women,

1386
00:55:42,339 --> 00:55:43,940
how they can dance
my themselves,

1387
00:55:43,973 --> 00:55:45,408
or with each other at a party,

1388
00:55:45,442 --> 00:55:47,644
and no one gives it a
second thought, you know.

1389
00:55:47,677 --> 00:55:49,079
I have to spend half the night

1390
00:55:49,112 --> 00:55:50,914
working up the courage
to ask a girl to dance,

1391
00:55:50,947 --> 00:55:52,248
and then if she rejects me,

1392
00:55:52,282 --> 00:55:53,583
I have to make
that shameful walk,

1393
00:55:53,616 --> 00:55:54,918
back across the room.

1394
00:55:54,951 --> 00:55:56,586
Then I get so depressed I just--

1395
00:55:56,619 --> 00:55:57,954
Joel. Joel. Joel.
Get a grip.

1396
00:55:57,987 --> 00:56:00,156
Have a waffle or something,
for crying out loud.

1397
00:56:02,292 --> 00:56:05,428
Could I get that
extra dressing I ordered?

1398
00:56:05,462 --> 00:56:07,263
Hey, my food! Hey, I'm
trying to eat over here!

1399
00:56:07,297 --> 00:56:08,631
Your feet smell like Fritos.

1400
00:56:08,665 --> 00:56:10,967
She's got a great act, really
knows how to work a table.

1401
00:56:12,836 --> 00:56:16,439
(grumbling) Now dessert.

1402
00:56:16,473 --> 00:56:17,607
(screaming)

1403
00:56:17,640 --> 00:56:18,675
Thanks. Good night.

1404
00:56:18,708 --> 00:56:20,610
Enjoy the buffet. (burping)

1405
00:56:20,643 --> 00:56:21,478
Carry-out.

1406
00:56:22,479 --> 00:56:23,780
Hey, come on over here.

1407
00:56:23,813 --> 00:56:24,848
Try a little of my
big tankard of beer.

1408
00:56:24,881 --> 00:56:26,216
Ah, come on.

1409
00:56:27,484 --> 00:56:29,986
Come on, try a potato
skin. They're great!

1410
00:56:33,156 --> 00:56:34,491
You have decaf, right?

1411
00:56:36,359 --> 00:56:39,028
You know what this
scene's making me hungry for?

1412
00:56:39,062 --> 00:56:41,464
- Me too.
- Waffles. Waffles.

1413
00:56:41,498 --> 00:56:42,499
Mmm.

1414
00:56:44,567 --> 00:56:45,702
(humming)

1415
00:56:45,735 --> 00:56:47,504
I gotta go. Excuse me.

1416
00:56:49,339 --> 00:56:51,040
I would like to
test the strength

1417
00:56:51,074 --> 00:56:53,309
of the great Viking women.

1418
00:56:53,343 --> 00:56:53,977
Me too.

1419
00:56:54,010 --> 00:56:55,378
I think it is a fraud.

1420
00:56:56,546 --> 00:56:58,982
Here, girl. Set this down.

1421
00:56:59,015 --> 00:57:01,885
Isn't that the pot
calling the kettle black?

1422
00:57:01,918 --> 00:57:03,153
You cad...

1423
00:57:03,186 --> 00:57:05,488
We're even now.
You're on your own.

1424
00:57:05,522 --> 00:57:07,023
(laughing)

1425
00:57:08,858 --> 00:57:12,195
I am Sprite of the
forest, friend to Oberon.

1426
00:57:12,228 --> 00:57:13,830
Merl Oberon, actually.

1427
00:57:13,863 --> 00:57:15,532
(laughing)

1428
00:57:17,700 --> 00:57:21,171
It's Blaze versus Weeny-man,
on American Gladiators.

1429
00:57:21,204 --> 00:57:24,107
It's actually Over the Top,
with Sly Stallone.

1430
00:57:27,410 --> 00:57:29,546
(groaning)

1431
00:57:30,380 --> 00:57:32,715
(laughing)

1432
00:57:36,219 --> 00:57:38,388
Okay, this one's for waffles.

1433
00:57:41,057 --> 00:57:42,692
Aren't you glad
you used Dial?

1434
00:57:42,725 --> 00:57:44,427
Don't you wish everybody did?

1435
00:57:45,762 --> 00:57:48,565
Ow! Ow! Ow! Ow!
You're harming me!

1436
00:57:49,933 --> 00:57:51,568
I don't believe it.

1437
00:57:53,570 --> 00:57:54,437
Enough!

1438
00:57:56,072 --> 00:57:57,740
You were adopted.

1439
00:57:59,075 --> 00:58:01,878
There is no need for any
contest with these women.

1440
00:58:01,911 --> 00:58:03,213
They are our slaves,

1441
00:58:03,246 --> 00:58:04,714
to do with as we please.

1442
00:58:04,747 --> 00:58:07,417
Oh, can we discuss art
and literature with them?

1443
00:58:09,652 --> 00:58:12,789
You, young man, are going
on a Robert Bly weekend.

1444
00:58:14,591 --> 00:58:15,625
(knocking)

1445
00:58:15,658 --> 00:58:17,093
Oh, it must be Thanksgiving.

1446
00:58:17,126 --> 00:58:22,765
- Oh, no, Jehovah's Witnesses.
- Hey, the floor-show started.

1447
00:58:22,799 --> 00:58:27,770
(dramatic music)

1448
00:58:28,938 --> 00:58:30,907
Great choreography.

1449
00:58:30,940 --> 00:58:32,275
He likes to hop.

1450
00:58:32,942 --> 00:58:33,776
Weee!

1451
00:58:34,777 --> 00:58:37,447
How about a little
fire, Scarecrow?

1452
00:58:37,480 --> 00:58:39,282
This is Todd, recreating
the role of Biff,

1453
00:58:39,315 --> 00:58:42,085
from West Side Story,
thank you very much.

1454
00:58:42,118 --> 00:58:43,453
Escape to the fitting room!

1455
00:58:44,954 --> 00:58:46,155
I fooled them.

1456
00:58:47,524 --> 00:58:48,625
Ah!

1457
00:58:50,493 --> 00:58:51,594
Joel?

1458
00:58:51,628 --> 00:58:54,464
What does this have
anything to do with waffles?

1459
00:58:54,497 --> 00:58:56,933
- I don't get it.
- Beats me.

1460
00:58:56,966 --> 00:58:58,434
L'Eggo my Eggo!

1461
00:58:58,468 --> 00:59:00,470
Oh, that's what has
to do with waffles.

1462
00:59:03,640 --> 00:59:04,674
Gob on him!

1463
00:59:04,707 --> 00:59:06,643
Dribble spit in his face!

1464
00:59:07,644 --> 00:59:08,478
(hocking)

1465
00:59:12,215 --> 00:59:15,118
Here's dessert, Krumkake.

1466
00:59:15,151 --> 00:59:16,185
(splashing)

1467
00:59:18,054 --> 00:59:19,355
Well, little man,

1468
00:59:19,389 --> 00:59:21,958
you seem to have a
taste for fighting.

1469
00:59:21,991 --> 00:59:25,028
I'll show you some Vikings
who have lost that desire.

1470
00:59:25,061 --> 00:59:26,796
Like Tarkenton,
he's kinda old.

1471
00:59:26,829 --> 00:59:29,365
And Rashad, he's got a
broadcasting career now.

1472
00:59:33,236 --> 00:59:34,637
Looks like they're
on a class field trip.

1473
00:59:34,671 --> 00:59:36,806
I used to love class
field trips, you know.

1474
00:59:36,839 --> 00:59:38,207
When I was a kid, you know,

1475
00:59:38,241 --> 00:59:40,310
one time I got sick
and I couldn't go.

1476
00:59:40,343 --> 00:59:41,844
The biggest day of
school of the year,

1477
00:59:41,878 --> 00:59:43,313
and I couldn't go.
(whistling)

1478
00:59:43,346 --> 00:59:46,149
They went up to the Gardner's
Soft-Twist bread factory.

1479
00:59:46,182 --> 00:59:47,517
(snoring)
And they they toured--

1480
00:59:47,550 --> 00:59:49,319
Joel, have a
waffle, will you?

1481
00:59:49,352 --> 00:59:51,354
- Shape up.
- Something chewy.

1482
00:59:54,691 --> 00:59:56,693
Jiminy.
You okay?

1483
01:00:00,263 --> 01:00:01,497
(whip cracking)

1484
01:00:01,531 --> 01:00:02,899
Ah, that's just
because I can.

1485
01:00:04,567 --> 01:00:06,569
Nice guard. Real
young guy there.

1486
01:00:10,206 --> 01:00:11,507
I love these walking scenes.

1487
01:00:11,541 --> 01:00:13,209
Oh, we missed
the Dead concert.

1488
01:00:13,242 --> 01:00:15,511
We try to amuse our guests.

1489
01:00:15,545 --> 01:00:17,547
But they're not always
grateful for our efforts.

1490
01:00:18,715 --> 01:00:20,350
Fortunately, that was
not one of your people.

1491
01:00:20,383 --> 01:00:22,085
He was with the Donner party.

1492
01:00:22,118 --> 01:00:23,353
The Richard Donner party.

1493
01:00:23,386 --> 01:00:25,021
Threw a bash for the
Lethal Weapon cast.

1494
01:00:25,054 --> 01:00:26,289
(laughing)

1495
01:00:28,391 --> 01:00:30,560
Oh, it's sculpture
class, look.

1496
01:00:31,461 --> 01:00:33,896
And they're doing
life studies there.

1497
01:00:33,930 --> 01:00:34,731
Studio arts.

1498
01:00:35,565 --> 01:00:36,799
Oh, Vedric.

1499
01:00:37,634 --> 01:00:39,535
Desir, are you real?

1500
01:00:39,569 --> 01:00:40,536
Brassiere?

1501
01:00:40,570 --> 01:00:41,537
Are you a dream?

1502
01:00:41,571 --> 01:00:43,606
Mary, Mary,
don't you know me?

1503
01:00:43,640 --> 01:00:47,043
They're slaves but they
do have to shave, look.

1504
01:00:47,076 --> 01:00:48,544
My love.

1505
01:00:48,578 --> 01:00:49,912
Yoman.

1506
01:00:49,946 --> 01:00:50,747
Yeoman?

1507
01:00:50,780 --> 01:00:51,714
Swaive!

1508
01:00:51,748 --> 01:00:52,915
Suede?

1509
01:00:52,949 --> 01:00:55,051
Oh, great! It's
an Abba reunion.

1510
01:00:55,084 --> 01:00:56,419
I was wondering.

1511
01:00:56,452 --> 01:01:00,423
Hey, how about a little
sugar for the Todd monster, hmm?

1512
01:01:00,456 --> 01:01:01,457
Come on.

1513
01:01:03,259 --> 01:01:04,594
I'm raven-haired and bitter.

1514
01:01:05,762 --> 01:01:08,431
My darling, what
have they done to you?

1515
01:01:08,464 --> 01:01:11,300
It doesn't matter.
You're here now.

1516
01:01:11,334 --> 01:01:15,304
Oh, jeez, he's a nice
well-groomed young man.

1517
01:01:15,338 --> 01:01:18,441
Quite touching, isn't it?

1518
01:01:20,777 --> 01:01:21,811
Separate them!

1519
01:01:21,844 --> 01:01:23,913
Colors in one pile
and whites in the other!

1520
01:01:23,946 --> 01:01:25,314
Cold water wash.

1521
01:01:27,016 --> 01:01:28,251
Alright, come on.

1522
01:01:28,284 --> 01:01:29,786
Conjugal visit is
over. Come with me.

1523
01:01:30,953 --> 01:01:32,288
Hey, wait!

1524
01:01:32,822 --> 01:01:34,424
She's just a friend.

1525
01:01:34,457 --> 01:01:35,992
We talk on the phone and stuff.

1526
01:01:37,960 --> 01:01:40,963
I smell a rat,
a big Viking rat.

1527
01:01:46,135 --> 01:01:47,937
So you scorn me.

1528
01:01:47,970 --> 01:01:50,139
Yet you're in the
arms of a slave.

1529
01:01:50,173 --> 01:01:52,608
- He's not a slave.
- He's a really nice guy.

1530
01:01:52,642 --> 01:01:55,945
Vedric is a warrior,
leader of the Stonyold.

1531
01:01:55,978 --> 01:01:57,146
The lives given to me by the

1532
01:01:57,180 --> 01:01:58,981
great serpent of
